window.AA_AAPQ_CARDS = [{"id": "aa-aapq-001", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module7", "modules": ["Module7"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 1", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 17, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 3</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-01-q-p003.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-01-q source PDF page 3\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 2</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-01-a-p002.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-01-a source PDF page 2\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 3</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-01-a-p003.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-01-a source PDF page 3\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 1 (17 marks) \nYou are the audit senior on the audit of the financial statements of Happy Flying Ltd for the year ended 30 \nApril 20X8. You are to perform the substantive analytical procedures of property, plant and equipment \n(‘PPE’) to assess the reasonableness of the depreciation charge for the year. \nYou have obtained the following information concerning PPE for the year ended 30 April 20X8. \n \nProperty\nNote 1\nPlant & Equipment\nNote 2\nComputer Equipment\n \n£’000\n£’000\n£’000\nCost or valuation at 30 April 20X8 \n36,000\n15,300\n950\nAccumulated depreciation at 1 May 20X7 \n4,560\n5,685\n400\nDepreciation for the year \n540\n2,115\n179\nDepreciation rate \n2% straight line\n15% straight line 33% diminishing balance\n1. The property portfolio was revalued on 31 October 20X7 by an independent professional firm and \nis now valued at £36,000k. Prior to the revaluation, the property portfolio was held at original cost \nof £27,000k. It was confirmed that the depreciation rate of 2% straight line was still deemed \nappropriate. \n2. From the review of the board minutes, you have noted that plant & equipment with a cost of \n£500,000 and fully written down at 30 April 20X7 was sold during the year and replaced by \n£950,000 of new plant & equipment. \nDifferences of over £76,000 are considered to be significant. \nRequired: \n1. Perform a substantive analytical review to analyse the reasonableness of the depreciation charge \nfor the year for each category of PPE. \n(9 marks) \n2. Analyse the reasons for variance between expectations and actual depreciation charge. \n(8 marks) \n(17 marks)", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 1 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 3</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-01-case-p003.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-01-case source PDF page 3\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [3], "solution": [2, 3]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-7"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-002", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module12", "modules": ["Module10", "Module12"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 2", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 37, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 4</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-02-q-p004.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-02-q source PDF page 4\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 4</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-02-a-p004.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-02-a source PDF page 4\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 5</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-02-a-p005.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-02-a source PDF page 5\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 6</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-02-a-p006.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-02-a source PDF page 6\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 2 (37 marks) \nIt is June 2X18 and you are the audit manager on the audit of the financial statements of Ship Ahoy \nClothes Ltd, (‘Ship’) a chain of 15 shops selling hand–crafted nautical clothes. Ship is a member of the \nExcelcior Ltd (‘Excelcior’) group and has been an audit client for several years. Your firm do not audit \nany other components of the Excelcior group but have undertaken other assurance engagements for \nits components in the past. \nShip has been experiencing significant trading difficulties caused by price competition from online \nsuppliers of similar products. \nYou are reviewing the matters contained in the audit highlights memorandum for the year ended 31 \nMarch 2X18 (Appendix 1), and your audit partner has asked you to advise on the potential impact on \nthe audit report and audit opinion of each matter, advising on whether it is appropriate to issue an \nunmodified audit report. You have also noted from a review of the audit file that Excelcior has not yet \nissued a letter of support to Ship. This has been provided in previous years. \nA few days ago the finance director from Berthing Ltd (Berthing), a member of the Excelior group \napproached your audit firm to request that it certify an accreditation return to the National Yacht and \nHarbour Association (NYHA). Your audit partner has provided a briefing note (Appendix 2) with further \ninformation on Berthing and the proposed engagement. He has asked you to prepare a memo \nexplaining the key elements to be considered in concluding whether this proposed engagement would \nbe classed as an assurance engagement and, if so, what level of assurance would be provided, so \nthat he can consider whether to accept and propose a suitable fee. \nRequired: \n1. Advise on the potential impact on the audit report and audit opinion of each of the above matters. \n(20 marks) \n2. Explain the key elements to be considered when concluding whether this assignment could be \nclassified as an assurance engagement  \n(10 marks) \n3. Explain the alternative levels of assurance that could be provided and recommend which level of \nassurance would be appropriate for this assignment.  \n(7 marks) \n(37 marks)\nAPPENDIX 1 (QUESTION 2) – extract from audit highlights \nmemorandum for Ship Ltd \nOverall materiality is £50,000 and Ship’s draft financial statements show a profit of £910,000. \n1. During the year the company exceeded its bank overdraft limit of £500,000. Review of \ncorrespondence from the bank indicated that the overdraft is unsecured, due for renewal on 1 \nSeptember 2X18 and that the bank is not willing to increase the facility. The bank is insisting that the \nbalance is brought back within the agreed limits. On 10 June 20X8 the overdraft was £533,092 per the \nbank statement. \n2. Following a VAT inspection by HMRC, several errors were identified where Ship had incorrectly \nclaimed input VAT. If HMRC decides to impose the maximum penalties and interest charges, the \ncompany will be liable for an additional payment to HMRC of £52,000. The tax department \nagrees with HMRC’s estimate, but the board has refused to accept this and have not provided for this \namount in the financial statements.\nAPPENDIX 2 (QUESTION 2) – briefing note from audit partner \nconcerning Berthing \nBerthing operates a marina occupying a two–acre site on the west coast of Wales. This provides pontoons \nfor berthing on the sea, refuelling, storage of boats on land, and laundry and toileting facilities for their \nmany customers. Berthing’s customers include commercial companies who make their living from the \nsea and private leisure boat owners. \nBerthing is working towards a national accreditation regulated by the NYHA. The accreditation is called the \n‘Gold Starfish’ accreditation. The achievement of the award is a significant achievement in the industry \nand would result in increased trade for the company. \nThe Gold Starfish accreditation is an environmental award that evaluates the company’s \nenvironmental policy and procedures and its environmental facilities. \nThere are rigorous standards which Berthing must achieve to obtain accreditation and these are all \ndetailed in the accreditation documentation. The environmental policy and procedures include the \nimplementation of guidelines for facility users on best practice of boating in relation to the environment. \nThe guidelines cover the implementation of waste recycling, oil and paint disposal, fuel spillage \ncontingency plans and sewerage pump–out facilities. \nAccreditation returns submitted to the NYHA require the attachment of specific supporting \ndocumentation by the applicant for the previous two years up to the end of May 2X18. The NYHA require \napplicants to obtain independent certification that the contents of this supporting documentation are \nmaterially correct. \nAdditionally, Berthing is required to keep documentation that details all incidents at the premises that have \nan environmental impact and how they were satisfactorily dealt with. The finance director highlighted \nthat there had been a fuel spillage from a fishing vessel and land contamination from a leaking oil tank. \nThese incidents have both occurred in the last two years. Both items are noted in Berthing’s \nenvironmental log which was introduced during December 2X16.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 2 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 4</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-02-case-p004.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-02-case source PDF page 4\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 5</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-02-case-p005.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-02-case source PDF page 5\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 6</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-02-case-p006.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-02-case source PDF page 6\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [4, 5, 6], "solution": [4, 5, 6]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "sourceNotice": "Source discrepancy: question heading states 37 marks; solution heading states 33 marks. Timer uses the question total. Source unchanged.", "linkTags": ["aa-module-10", "aa-module-12"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-003", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module5", "modules": ["Module5"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 3", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 16, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 7</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-03-q-p007.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-03-q source PDF page 7\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 7</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-03-a-p007.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-03-a source PDF page 7\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 8</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-03-a-p008.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-03-a source PDF page 8\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 3 (16 marks) \nIt is July 20X4. You are an audit manager with Rubicon & Co (‘Rubicon’), a firm of registered auditors. \nYou are responsible for the audit of Caring Medical Supplies Group (‘the Group’) and are planning the audit \nfor the year ending 30 November 20X4. Rubicon audits all the group companies. \nAt present, there are two companies within the group. Caring Medical Supplies plc (‘Caring’) is the holding \ncompany and operates from the group headquarters in Edinburgh. Caring controls the statutory \nadministration for the group and levies management charges on the subsidiary. Caring Surgical Ltd \n(‘Surgical’) is a wholly owned subsidiary engaged in the design and sale of surgical equipment. \nThe group is engaged in the worldwide design and sale of surgical equipment and the sale of other medical \nsupplies and equipment. The group benefits greatly from the research of Dr Sharon Brown, the \ntechnical director, who is a leading specialist in the field of developing surgical equipment. Caring \ndesigns the surgical equipment and once the design is approved by the UK, EU and American authorities, it \nis sold by Caring to a US manufacturer, Fishers Inc (‘Fishers’), of which Dr Sharon Brown is also a \ndirector and majority shareholder. Fishers adds its expertise to complete the detailed specification and \nmanufacture the equipment. Fishers sells the equipment in non–European markets. Caring has a licence \nto sell the equipment in the UK & Europe and receives commission for the sale of each unit of equipment \nwhen it is sold. \nThe design of the equipment and subsequent sales in the UK & Europe are made by Surgical. Surgical \nis responsible for the marketing of the equipment worldwide and the sale commissions received from \nFishers for each unit sold reflects this. \nThe audit partner, Adam, has advised you that the audit team had problems in previous years auditing \nrelated party transactions. Because of this, and his understanding of the transactions, he considers there to \nbe a high risk of material misstatement in relation to the identification of related parties and management’s \naccounting for and disclosure of related parties. He would like you to provide him with a list of procedures to \nallow the audit team to obtain an understanding of, and to confirm the completeness of, related parties \nidentified for the purposes of the audit. He would also like you to design substantive procedures to test \nmanagement’s accounting and disclosure of related party transactions. \nRequired: \nDesign the audit procedures required to allow the audit team to: \na) Obtain an understanding of and to confirm the completeness of related parties identified for the \npurposes of the audit; and \n(8 marks) \nb) Test management’s accounting and disclosure of related party transactions \n(8 marks) \n(16 marks)", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 3 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 7</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-03-case-p007.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-03-case source PDF page 7\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [7], "solution": [7, 8]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-5"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-004", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module2", "modules": ["Module2"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 4", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 25, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 8</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-04-q-p008.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-04-q source PDF page 8\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 9</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-04-a-p009.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-04-a source PDF page 9\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 10</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-04-a-p010.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-04-a source PDF page 10\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 4 (25 marks) \nIt is early in July X9. You are a recently qualified CA who trained at an audit firm but who now works in \nthe finance team of Lamb Fermented Beverages Group plc (‘Lamb’), a large brewery group. \nLamb recently acquired a 100% shareholding in small, rural craft brewery in Yorkshire, England, called \nProctor Brews Ltd (‘Proctor’), to enhance Lamb ’s product portfolio. \nA due diligence review during the acquisition process raised some concerns over Proctor’s financial \nadministration and asset management. This review, combined with the wider upheaval of the \ntakeover, prompted Proctor’s financial controller to retire from their role.  \nProctor’s financial reporting year ended on 30 June X9. The process for preparing Proctor’s financial \nstatements has not yet begun and, considering the concerns raised by the recent review and the loss \nof the financial controller, your manager has asked you to urgently assess the accuracy, validity and \ncompleteness of Proctor’s data relating to property, plant and equipment (‘PPE’). They have also \nasked you to conclude on whether the data is appropriate for use in preparing Proctor’s financial \nstatements for the year ending 30 June 20X4, and the subsequent audit of these financial statements. \nYou have begun by extracting a sample of PPE assets from Proctor’s Fixed Asset Register (‘FAR’) as \nat 30 June 20X9 (Appendix 1). You have also found  related documents at Proctor’s offices. These \ndocuments, and some other information you have sourced yourself, are at Appendix 2. These include: \n1. Proctor Policy – Accounting for tangible non–current assets  \n2. Email from Proctor estates manager \n3. Email from Proctor fleet manager \n4. Three invoices \n5. GBP:EUR exchange rate (notes) \n6. Analysis of miscellaneous expenses \nRequired: \nPerform procedures to confirm the accuracy, validity and completeness of Proctors’ data relating to its \nPPE assets. Document and evaluate the results of your procedures, including any issues identified \nwith the data. \nForm an overall conclusion as to whether the data is appropriate for use in the preparation of Proctor’s \nfinancial statements for the year to 30 June 20X4 and the subsequent audit of these financial \nstatements. \nYou are not required to identify any further evidence or work that should be performed. \n(25 marks)\nAppendix 1 – Sample Extract of Proctor FAR1 as at 30 June X9 \nAsset \nreference \nAsset \nDescription \nDate of \npurchase \nCost \nUseful life \nEstimated \nresidual \nvalue \nAccumulated \ndepreciation \nand impairment \nCarrying \namount brought \nforward \nDepreciation \ncharge in \n20X8–9 \nImpairment \ncharge in \n20X8–9 \nCarrying \namount as at \n30 June X9 \nAR10012 \nWarehouse \nbuilding A \n01/07/X0 \n£203,000 \n25 years \n£0 \n£64,960 \n£138,040 \n£5,522 \nnil \n£132,518 \nAR10114 \nLorry #13 \n12/5/X6 \n£72,000 \n5 years \n£14,400 \n£24,960 \n£47,040 \n£11,520 \nnil \n£35,520 \nAR10164 \nAtlas light \nvan #9 \n01/06/X9 \n£24,000 \n10 years \n£2,400 \nNil – addition \nNil – addition \n£180 \nnil \n£23,820 \nAR10167 \nConveyor belt, \nBottling Plant 1 \n23/06/X9 \n£96,000 \n10 years \n£10,000 \nNil – addition \nNil – addition \n£717 \nnil \n£95,283 \nAR10169 \nGas injector, \nBottling Plant 2 \n30/06/X9 \n£51,170 \n10 years \n£5,000 \nNil – addition \nNil – addition \n£1,924 \nnil \n£49,246 \n \n \n1 The FAR is a module of a market–leading accountancy software package. You can assume it is arithmetically reliable throughout ie: depreciation charges, accumulation of depreciation, and \ncasting are all calculated reliably, based on the asset data held.\nAppendix 2 – Other information \n1. Proctor Policy – Accounting for tangible non–current assets \nThis policy applies to all tangible non–current assets held by Proctor. \nA \nInitial Recognition \nA non–current asset shall be recognised when: \n• \nIt is probable that future economic benefits associated with the asset will begin to flow to the \nentity. \n• \nThe cost of the asset can be measured reliably. \n• \nProctor recognises a Right of Use Asset for vehicles etc that are leased but not owned by \nProctor. See the related Proctor Policy ‘IFRS 16’ for further details [not attached]. Proctor does \nnot enter any low value/short term lease agreements. \nIncluded Costs \n• \nPurchase price (net of discounts and rebates). \n• \nDirectly attributable costs to bring the asset to its intended location and condition (e.g. \ndelivery, installation, professional fees). \nExcluded Costs \n• \nAdministration and general overheads. \n• \nCosts incurred after the asset is ready for use (e.g. training staff, marketing). \n• \nVAT (all Proctor assets are assumed to be recoverable for VAT purposes) \nB \nSubsequent Measurement \nAssets are measured using the cost model – cost less accumulated depreciation and impairment \nlosses. \nC Depreciation \nDepreciation is charged systematically over the asset’s useful life: \n• \nBegins when the asset is available for use. \n• \nEnds when the asset is derecognised or classified as held for sale. \n• \nMethod and useful life are reviewed annually. \n• \nAll Proctor’s depreciable assets are depreciated on a ‘straight line’ basis. The useful lives are: \n• \nBuildings: 25 years \n• \nProduction machinery and equipment: 10 years \n• \nVehicles: 5 years \nDepreciation is charged monthly. Depreciation is charged in the month of acquisition but not in the \nmonth of disposal. \nD Impairment \nAssets are tested for impairment when indicators suggest the carrying amount may not be \nrecoverable: \n• \nRecoverable amount is the higher of fair value less costs to sell and value in use. \n• \nImpairment losses are recognised in profit or loss.\nAPPENDIX 2 (Question 4) (continued) \nE \nDisposals \nAn asset is derecognised on disposal or when no future economic benefits are expected: \n• \nGain or loss on disposal = proceeds – carrying amount. \n• \nRecognised in profit or loss. \n2. Email from Proctor estates manager \nFrom: estates@proctorbrews.co.uk \nTo: finance@proctorbrews.co.uk \nDate: 31/05/X9 \nSubject: Washout \nHi everyone! Thought I should give you an update on the river flood that affected our premises last \nnight. \nWork is ongoing to remove silt and debris from the yard. There was also flooding and contamination of \nWarehouse A.  We are also concerned that its south wall, overlooking the river, may have been \nundermined by riverbank erosion. All a bit of a mess while we assess the damage and try to work out \nhow long it’s going to affect normal operations. You’ll want to get in touch with the warehouse team to \nget an estimate of inventory write–offs and the costs of temporary off–site storage. \nAll the best \nAlice \n3. Email from Proctor Fleet Manager \nFrom: fleetmanagement@proctorbrews.co.uk \nTo: finance@proctorbrews.co.uk \nDate: 29 April X9 \nSubject: Disposal of lorries \nHi team, hope you are all well. Just to let you know we have disposed of two lorries, #12 and #14. \nThese were acquired for £72k each back in May X6. As the company has grown the fleet has been \nworked much harder than anticipated. They were getting increasingly expensive to keep on the road, \nso it was time to let them go. We got £20,000 in total from our usual trade dealer. \nHope that’s enough information for you to process things – let me know if you need more. \nJack\nAPPENDIX 2 (Question 4) (continued) \n4. Invoice \n5. Invoice \nInvoice \nProctor Brews Ltd \nYorkshire \nEngland \nInvoice number: 1010 \nDate: 01/06/X9 \nValue (£) \n   VAT \nFor supply and delivery today of 2 units, ‘Atlas’ light van     40,000      8,000 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nTOTAL               \n       48,000 \n \n \nHirsch Vehicle Supplies ltd \nYorkshire, England \nInvoice \nProctor Brews Ltd \nYorkshire \nEngland \nInvoice number: 21177 \nDate: 23/06/X9 \nFor supply, installation, and testing of ‘Trustybelt V’ conveyor belt, Proctor Bottling \nPlant 1. Installation and testing to be completed, week commencing 7/7/X9. \nValue (£) \n   VAT \n   96,000      19,200 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nTOTAL \n \n      115,200 \n \nAs agreed, payment in full is required in advance of works commencing on site.  \nB.L. Systèmes de Gaz SARL \nDieppe, FranceP\nAPPENDIX 2 (Question 4) (continued) \n6. Invoice \n7. GBP:EUR exchange rate (notes) \nThe following exchange rate was obtained and agreed reasonable based on an independent third–\nparty source: \nGBP:EUR, 30/6/X9 = 1.190 \n8. Analysis of Miscellaneous Expenses \nExtract of amounts applied to ‘miscellaneous expenses’ general ledger code, over £2,000, in month \nending 30/6/X9: \nDate \nValue \nNarrative \n02/06/X9 \n£3,500.00 \nAuditor’s fees – interim audit visit in May \n11/06/X9 \n£4,750.00 \nSolicitors – advice to directors re takeover bid \n17/06/X9 \n£2,300.00 \nBrewery storage tanks – monthly leasing charge \n28/06/X9 \n£2,000.00 \nExternal caterers – staff summer party \nInvoice \nProctor Brews Ltd \nYorkshire \nEngland \nInvoice number: 1614 \nDate: 30/06/X9 \nValue (€) \n   VAT \nFor supply, installation, and testing of ‘Geant Gazeuse 3’      43,000      \n8,600 \ngas injector system in Bottling Plant 2. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nTOTAL                             51,600 \n \nWork completed at today’s date. \n \nB.L. Systèmes de Gaz SARL \nDieppe, France", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 4 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 8</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-04-case-p008.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-04-case source PDF page 8\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 9</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-04-case-p009.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-04-case source PDF page 9\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 10</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-04-case-p010.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-04-case source PDF page 10\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 11</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-04-case-p011.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-04-case source PDF page 11\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 12</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-04-case-p012.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-04-case source PDF page 12\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 13</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-04-case-p013.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-04-case source PDF page 13\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13], "solution": [9, 10]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "sourceNotice": "Source discrepancy: question heading states 25 marks; solution heading states 30 marks. Timer uses the question total. Source unchanged.", "linkTags": ["aa-module-2"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-005", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module8", "modules": ["Module4", "Module6", "Module8"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 5", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 47, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 14</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-05-q-p014.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-05-q source PDF page 14\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 11</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-05-a-p011.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-05-a source PDF page 11\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 12</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-05-a-p012.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-05-a source PDF page 12\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 13</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-05-a-p013.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-05-a source PDF page 13\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 14</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-05-a-p014.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-05-a source PDF page 14\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 5 (47 marks) \nYou are the audit manager on the external audit of the financial statements of Icarus Failsafe Systems \nLtd (‘Icarus’). Icarus prepares its financial statements under International Financial Reporting \nStandards. \nIcarus is involved in the ‘green’ energy sector. It operates out of three geographically diverse factories, \nbased in Glasgow, Inverness and Gloucester. Over the last four years the company has grown \nsubstantially, mainly from manufacturing, supplying and installing solar panels for domestic properties. \nYou are planning the audit for the year ending 31 December 20X7, and considering the approach to \nproperty, plant and equipment (‘PPE’). \nYou have held a preliminary meeting with Ally McIntyre (‘Ally’), Icarus’s finance director. At your \nmeeting, Ally explained that the company continues to invest in equipment in all its factories. During \n20X7, the company has started to purchase new digital equipment, which is being acquired, for the \nfirst time, through leases and these will be phased in over the next three years, rather than being \ncompletely replaced in the current year. \nTo complete the audit planning memorandum, you need to draft paragraphs analysing and explaining \nthe audit risks in relation to PPE. Ally has provided you with extracts from the management accounts, \nfor the period ended 30 June 20X7 relating to PPE, and some additional comments (Appendix 1). \nIn January 20X7 the company implemented a new purchasing system for PPE. From the audit of prior \nyears, you are aware that control systems within Icarus are generally satisfactory, and you rely \nextensively on the controls in your audit work. From your discussions with Ally and your review of the \nsystem notes provided (Appendix 2), you are happy that the new system is well–designed, and you \nare, therefore, going to design the tests of control that your firm will perform for PPE. \nOn the assumption that the results of these tests will be satisfactory you are also going to design \nspecific substantive audit procedures for inclusion in a PPE substantive testing programme to respond \nto the audit risks identified during your work on the audit planning memorandum. \nRequired: \n1. Evaluate the audit risks in relation to PPE.  \n(13 marks) \n2. Design the tests of control that your firm would perform on the new purchasing system for PPE. \n(23 marks) \n3. Design procedures for inclusion in a substantive testing audit work programme for PPE, to \naddress each audit risk relating to PPE identified above. \n(11 marks) \n(47 Marks)\nAPPENDIX 1 (QUESTION 5) – Extract from management accounts \nProperty, plant and equipment \n \nLand &\nBuildings\nPlant &\nEquipment\nMotor Vehicles\nTotal\n \n£’000\n£’000\n£’000\n£’000\nCost or valuation \nAs at 1 January 20X7 \n1,765\n2,850\n780\n5,395\nRevaluation surplus \n500\n–\n–\n500\nAdditions \n–\n750\n250\n1,000\nDisposals \n–\n(400)\n(200)\n(600)\nAs at 30 June 20X7 \n2,265\n3,200\n830\n6,295\nAccumulated depreciation \nAs at 1 January 20X7 \n445\n2,000\n330\n2,775\nDepreciation charge \n15\n150\n100\n265\nDepreciation on disposals \n–\n(300)\n(150)\n(450)\nAs at 30 June 20X7 \n460\n1,850\n280\n2,590\nNet book value \nAs at 30 June 20X7 \n1,805\n1,350\n550\n3,705\nAs at 31 December 20X6 \n1,320\n850\n450\n2,620\nFor the first time, Icarus has leased digital plant and machinery and office equipment. The assets \nsubject to lease have all been capitalised. The total capital amount outstanding at 30 June 20X7 is \n£245,000. \nAdditions to motor vehicles, in January 20X7, included a Porsche car which belonged to the partner of \nIcarus’s chief executive. It is deemed to be worth at least £50,000, however they have not yet been \npaid for the vehicle. \nOld digital plant and equipment was disposed of as scrap for £20,000. It was previously anticipated \nthat these assets had a remaining useful life of four years. \nDepreciation is provided to write off the cost of assets over their useful lives on a straight–line basis. \nThe annual rates used are: \n \n%\nLand \nNil\nBuildings \n2\nPlant & Machinery \n25\nFixtures & Fittings \n10\nMotor vehicles \n25\nLeased plant \nOver period of lease\nOn 31 March 20X7, the board obtained an independent valuation of its factories in Glasgow, Inverness \nand Gloucester. \nThe independent valuation provided a valuation of £1,130,000 for the factories in Glasgow and Inverness. \nAs at 31 March 20X7 the two factories had a net book value of £630,000. The valuation of the Gloucester \nfactory showed no change in value – this is in line with market expectation of property prices in the area.\nAPPENDIX 2 (QUESTION 5) – Notes on new purchasing system \nsupplied by Ally McIntyre \nRequisitioning \nAs part of the annual budget process, all departments (‘source departments’) are requested to submit \ncapital expenditure plans, approved by the relevant head of department. These plans are examined by \nthe board and items approved or refused as the board decides. All items costing \n£10,000 or more are required to be individually approved by the board. For items costing less than \nthis, each source department is given an overall allocation and it is the responsibility of the source \ndepartment head to stay within the budget. \nThe minutes of board meetings show the capital expenditure items approved, and from these the \naccounts department prepares an electronic schedule of approved items (‘the schedule’) which is \nchecked by Ally McIntyre. The schedule is held on a password protected spreadsheet, within the \naccounts department, under the control of James Summerville, accounts manager. \nDuring the year, there may be unbudgeted capital expenditure. In this situation the source department \nhead prepares a case for the expenditure, which is presented to the board for approval. The schedule \nis amended to reflect these changes on a monthly basis. A printout of amendments is retained by the \naccounts department. Ally checks these have been implemented and signs them to evidence this \ncheck. \nWhen capital expenditure has been approved, Ally decides how the expenditure is to be funded – by \noutright purchase or lease depending on the immediate impact on cashflow. In the case of major \nexpenditure, specific loan funding may be required. He advises James of the decision, and the funding \nmethod is entered in the schedule. \nOrdering \nThe head of the source department sends a requisition to James, who compares it with the schedule \nof approved items. If these agree, one of his two clerks raises a pre–numbered three– part order. The \nfirst two copies are sent to the supplier and the source department respectively and the third copy is \nsigned, as authorised, by James and filed by the accounts department in the approved items file. \nEach month, James sends the board a report of all orders placed. Purchases greater than £10,000 are \nindividually identified and those under that amount are grouped together for each source department. \nThis report is examined by the board, compared with the schedule of approved items, and minuted. \nPurchase order numbers and the purchase price are entered in the schedule of approved items file, \nwhich also identifies any differences between the amounts approved by the board and the actual \npurchase price. These differences are investigated by Ally and form part of the monthly board \ndiscussions. \nReceipt of asset \nAssets are delivered to the source department, compared with the order and checked for condition, \nspecification etc. The order is then matched with the supplier’s delivery note, which is retained by the \nsource department head until the invoice is received.\nAPPENDIX 2 (QUESTION 5) (Continued) \nApproval and processing of invoices \nPurchase invoices are received by the accounts department, recorded in a computerised invoice \nregister, numbered and sent to the source department head for approval. The invoice numbers are \nnoted in the invoice register and any invoices not returned from the source department within two \nweeks are chased up by the accounts department. The department head compares the invoice with \nthe order, signs the invoice and returns it with the delivery note to the accounts department. Invoices \nare then given nominal and purchase ledger codes by the accounts department. \nGermaine Green, the financial accountant, checks codes on the numbered invoices, agrees them with \ndelivery notes and purchase orders and signs the invoices for processing. \nPurchase invoices are processed on a daily basis in numerical order. The integrated computerised \nsystem updates the nominal and purchase ledgers and produces a journal which is used to manually \nupdate the fixed asset register (‘FAR’). There should be no differences between the FAR and the \nnominal ledger, but the system produces a monthly exception report confirming agreement or \nidentifying any differences. These reports are approved by James and retained in the accounts \ndepartment. \nIf the asset is acquired by lease, Germaine takes responsibility for arranging the contract documents. \nDetails of the contracts and payment information are given to James for matching with orders and \ndelivery notes in the normal way. All lease contracts are approved by Ally.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 5 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 14</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-05-case-p014.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-05-case source PDF page 14\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 15</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-05-case-p015.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-05-case source PDF page 15\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 16</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-05-case-p016.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-05-case source PDF page 16\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 17</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-05-case-p017.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-05-case source PDF page 17\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [14, 15, 16, 17], "solution": [11, 12, 13, 14]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-4", "aa-module-6", "aa-module-8"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-006", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module6", "modules": ["Module6"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 6", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 50, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 18</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-06-q-p018.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-06-q source PDF page 18\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 15</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-06-a-p015.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-06-a source PDF page 15\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 16</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-06-a-p016.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-06-a source PDF page 16\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 17</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-06-a-p017.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-06-a source PDF page 17\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 6 (50 marks) \nYou are a consultant specialising in e–commerce and IT controls and have been engaged by a \nrecently established firm of insurance brokers called E–Brokersinsurance4U Ltd (‘E–Brokers’). The \ndirectors have asked you to evaluate their web–based sales system and the general IT control \nenvironment to identify weaknesses within each. They would like you to explain the impact of each \nweakness on the business and to recommend relevant controls to mitigate the impact of each of these \nweaknesses. \nE–Brokers is an online and telephone–based insurance brokerage, based in Manchester, specialising \nin car, home and pet insurance. The company acts as an intermediary for four large insurance \ncompanies and deals with them exclusively. The company was established six months ago and its \nowner and managing director is Jack MacKay, a former employee of a large nationwide insurance \ncompany. \nJack’s vision for E–Brokers is for it to be a completely paperless environment with all information \nstored and processed electronically. This includes information obtained from customers via telephone \nenquiries and quotations from insurance companies. \nDue to the nature of E–Brokers’ business it is regulated by the Financial Conduct Authority (‘FCA’). \nProcedures were set up prior to the start of operations to satisfy the FCA authorisation process and \nthese are the ones that have been adhered to since the business started trading. Jack needs to \nensure that the appropriate regulatory requirements continue being adhered to, particularly in relation \nto the retention of customer data as a lack of controls and correct documentation in this area could \nresult in large fines from the FCA. E–Brokers currently employs 16 members of staff including Steven \nSmith, the company’s IT Manager. You met recently with Steven to discuss the general IT control \nenvironment and the  sales system of the company. Notes from your meeting are included in the \nAppendix. \nRequired: \n1. Identify the weaknesses in the general IT control environment, explain the implications of each \nweakness and recommend improvements. \n(20 marks) \n2. Identify the weaknesses in the sales system, explain the implications of each weakness and \nrecommend improvements. \n(30 marks) \n(50 marks)\nAPPENDIX (QUESTION 6) – Notes from meeting with Steven Smith, \nE–Brokers IT Manager \nIT department and systems \nThe IT department currently consists of Steven and two other employees. Steven has a list of the \nprocedures that are required for the FCA regulations, and these are incorporated in E–Brokers IT \nstrategy. \nE–Brokers’ IT system is a fully integrated bespoke package designed by external software developers. \nIt is built around a Customer Relationship Management system as the main business of E–Brokers is \nto sell insurance policies. It is integrated with the company’s financial system and links to the \ntelephone system allowing all calls to and from customers to be logged. Since implementation of the \nbespoke package, when the company started trading six months ago, the software developers have \nnot worked on site. Limited systems documentation was left for Steven and system support is \navailable via a telephone helpline. \nInterfaces \nThere is an interface in place with the bank which allows customer debit and credit card details to be \nchecked. In addition to this interface there are also direct interfaces in place with the four insurance \ncompanies for whom E–Brokers acts as an intermediary. During the year several customer files were \ncorrupted by a virus. E–Brokers believes that this could only have come from one of the interfaces, as \nthe security processes at the interface had not been reviewed or upgraded for one year. \nWhen customers log their details on the E–Brokers website, the information is fed to the four partner \ninsurance companies who offer quotes to E–Brokers. These quotes are passed on to customers at a \nmarked up price. Information therefore flows from E–Brokers to the insurance companies and also \nflows back from the insurance companies to E–Brokers and then onwards to the customer. This is a \nfully automated process which takes a matter of seconds. \nSystem Reliability \nThere have been several periods of downtime since the system went live six months ago. Steven is \nunsure what the root cause of the downtime is. During periods of downtime, customers cannot access \nthe internet site and brokers cannot record any customer information received during telephone calls. \nIn addition, they cannot obtain quotes from insurance companies or buy policies. Staff are frustrated \nby these periods of system downtime. \nBackups \nThe system is backed up on a daily basis with files stored on password protected cloud drive. \nHowever, due to storage space restrictions, the backup has not been performed in 2 months.  \nDisaster Recovery \nSteven noted that he had drafted an IT risk assessment, but it had not yet been reviewed by Jack, \nalthough it was on his to–do list to be reviewed by the end of the year. Based on the first six months \ntrading, system downtime is the highest–level risk. \nA disaster recovery plan is in place, which has recently been tested successfully and communicated to \nstaff.\nAPPENDIX (QUESTION 6) (Continued) \nSales System \nSales website \nThere is a website element to the system where customers can buy and manage their policies online. \nNew customers that register with E–Brokers are given a unique username by the system designed by \nusing a combination of their surname and date of birth. Each customer is then asked to create their \nown alphanumerical password to access their file. Website customers have the option to contact the \ncompany by telephone. \nCustomers are not prompted to change their passwords after initial set up. E–Brokers does not keep a \nrecord of multiple attempts to access customers’ accounts. During the year a customer queried \nchanges that had been made to their account without their knowledge and following investigations the \ncompany determined that a third party had accessed this account. No other instances have been \nnotified by customers. \nObtaining a quote for insurance \nOnce customers have logged into the system, they are able to select the type of insurance they want \nto purchase: car, pet or home insurance. They complete the online questionnaire and on completion \nare given a quote or are recommended to telephone the company if their request is unusual. \nSales \nIf the customer proceeds to buy the policy, they must tick a box to confirm they agree to the terms and \nconditions. Customers cannot purchase a policy without a valid e–mail address as all documentation, \nincluding policy documents, is e–mailed to them in a secure un–editable format, similar to a PDF \nformat, in order to maintain a paperless environment and manage costs. \nAny documents that require a customer’s signature are issued with instructions to print off, sign and \nreturn to the company by post, or be accepted with an electronic signature. \nAn automatic e–mail is sent to customers who have not returned signed documents within 10 days of \nissue asking for the documents to be returned as soon as possible. After 15 days the customer details \nare included in an exception report automatically produced daily which highlights outstanding \ndocuments requiring a signature. E–Broker’s insurance executives telephone these customers to \nprompt the return of the documents. Paper signed documents returned from customers are scanned \nimmediately upon receipt and saved to the customer’s electronic file by whoever opens the mail. \nThe original documents are shredded and not reviewed by the insurance staff. This sometimes causes \nproblems when there are claims on an insurance policy and the scanned originals cannot be located. \nPayment of premiums \nCustomers purchasing policies via the website have two options for making payments. They can either \npay by debit or credit card, or pay by direct debit over 12 months. They must enter card details which \nare verified through a link to the “Verified by Visa” system and other equivalent systems, or, for direct \ndebits, enter bank account details. The system will not allow the purchase to be finalised until either of \nthese sections is completed and the details verified. This is carried out through the bank interface for \nthe verification to be completed. Revenue is recognised for accounting purposes as soon as the policy \npurchase is finalised.\nAPPENDIX (QUESTION 6) (Continued) \nTelephone \nNew telephone customers register with E–Brokers providing their full name, postal address, including \npost code, contact telephone number and valid e–mail address. Following set up of the customer \naccount, the company’s brokers access customer files on the computer using the customer’s full name \ngiven by the customer over the phone. They recognise the customer on the phone from the personal \nrelationship they develop. \nPayment for policies can be made by providing debit or credit card details to a broker by telephone \nand these are logged directly into the system by the broker and stored in the customer’s electronic file. \nThe company only accepts payment by this method and does not accept payment by cheque. \nCommission \nThere are three brokers, one responsible for each type of insurance sold by E–Brokers, car, pet or \nhome. Each broker receives a commission payment for all policies sold in the category they are \nresponsible for. \nThe system automatically calculates the commission earned by each broker based on policies \npurchased from the partner insurance companies and sold on to customers. This commission is \nautomatically posted into the accounting system once calculated without being reviewed. Brokers are \nautomatically paid commission on a monthly basis for all policies sold in the calendar month prior to \nthe commission payment being made. \nRevenue is also calculated automatically and posted to the accounting system based on the insurance \npolicies sold during the month", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 6 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 18</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-06-case-p018.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-06-case source PDF page 18\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 19</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-06-case-p019.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-06-case source PDF page 19\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 20</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-06-case-p020.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-06-case source PDF page 20\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 21</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-06-case-p021.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-06-case source PDF page 21\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [18, 19, 20, 21], "solution": [15, 16, 17]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-6"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-007", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module9", "modules": ["Module8", "Module9"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 7", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 26, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 22</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-07-q-p022.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-07-q source PDF page 22\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 18</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-07-a-p018.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-07-a source PDF page 18\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 19</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-07-a-p019.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-07-a source PDF page 19\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 20</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-07-a-p020.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-07-a source PDF page 20\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 7 (26 marks) \nYou are the audit senior working at Donald and Mickey LLP, a medium–sized audit firm. You are \ncurrently working on the audit of Macivie Construction Group Plc. The audit manager has asked for \nyour assistance in designing substantive procedures for two of the group subsidiaries Lochmabie \nBuilding Ltd (‘Lochmabie’) and Barlow Ltd (‘Barlow’). Both subsidiaries have a year end of 31 July \n20X6. \nLochmabie is a property construction company specialising in the development of commercial \nbuildings for business and social use. \nYou audit fieldwork for Lochmabie is almost complete and your audit manager has given you a list of \nmatters arising from the audit that may require provision within the financial statements or disclosure \nas contingent liabilities (Appendix 1). You have been asked to design substantive procedures to allow \nthe audit senior to gather sufficient evidence for you to make this assessment. \nBarlow is primarily a property management company, but due to the poor performance of the property \nmarket in the last few years, the company started investing surplus cash in an equity investment \nportfolio. You are about to complete the substantive audit testing over the company’s investments and \nneed to finalise the audit procedures to be conducted. Background information on Barlow’s investment \nportfolio has been obtained from the subsidiary’s financial controller (Appendix 2). Your audit manager \nhas asked you to design audit procedures to test the investments balance held by Barlow. Please note \nthat the firm’s audit software automatically generates standard tests, so these do not need to be \nincluded. \nRequired: \n1. Design a list of substantive procedures to allow the audit senior to gather sufficient evidence on \nthe matters arising, to allow you to assess the need for any required provision or contingent \nliability disclosures within the financial statements. \n(15 marks) \n2. Design a list of substantive audit procedures to test the investments balance held by Barlow as at \n31 July 20X6 \n(11 marks) \n(26 marks)\nAPPENDIX 1 (QUESTION 7) – Matters arising from audit of \nLochmabie \nThe matters arising are: \n1. An employee of Lochmabie was involved in a work–related accident during the construction of a \nnew health centre. The accident is currently under investigation and it is the subject of ongoing \nlegal action, as the employee is suing the company for negligence based on a claim of being \nprovided with faulty safety equipment. \n2. Lochmabie is currently subject to an environmental investigation from a local regulatory body \nconcerning damage to protected plant life during building works from January to April 20X6. \nLochmabie has highlighted that it is aware of the plant life in question but has obtained assurance \nthat no damage took place during building activities. \n3. Included within the draft financial statements is a warranty provision intended to cover the cost of \nclaims made on building work performed in a previous two–year period. The provision is currently \nstated at £50,000 which is well in excess of claims in previous years (20X5: \n£36,000, 20X4: £41,000 and 20X3: £27,000). In the year to 31 July 20X6, warranty claims made \nwere £89,000. \n4. Lochmabie built a small development of eight houses in Fort William, Scotland, during the year \nand ran a promotion whereby if any of the owners wished to carry out further building work over a \nvalue of £10,000, they would receive cash–back of 2% of the purchase price, if they appointed \nLochmabie to complete the work. The houses cost £300,000 each and the revenue was \nrecognised in full when the properties legally transferred to the new owners. The promotion \napplied only to this development of eight houses.\nAPPENDIX 2 (QUESTION 7) – Information on Barlow investment \nportfolio provided by Barlow financial controller \nBarlow has entered into multiple transactions in relation to its investment during the year. All \ninvestments are in listed shares. \nBarlow classifies investments at fair value (‘FV’) with any adjustments processed as required following \nreview. \nYou have obtained the following information from the company’s financial controller, detailing the \nmovements in Barlow’s investment portfolio. \nInvestments: \nInvestments\n£000\nOpening balance at 1 August 20X5 \n25\nAdditions \n120\nDisposals \n–\nFV adjustment \n10\nClosing balance at 31 July 20X6 \n155", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 7 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 22</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-07-case-p022.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-07-case source PDF page 22\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 23</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-07-case-p023.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-07-case source PDF page 23\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 24</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-07-case-p024.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-07-case source PDF page 24\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [22, 23, 24], "solution": [18, 19, 20]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-8", "aa-module-9"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-008", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module10", "modules": ["Module10"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 8", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 48, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 25</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-08-q-p025.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-08-q source PDF page 25\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 21</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-08-a-p021.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-08-a source PDF page 21\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 22</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-08-a-p022.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-08-a source PDF page 22\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 23</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-08-a-p023.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-08-a source PDF page 23\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 24</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-08-a-p024.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-08-a source PDF page 24\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "QUESTION 8 (48 marks) \nYou are the audit manager on the audit of the financial statements of Airline Braking Systems Ltd \n(‘Braking’) for the year ended 31 March 20X1. Following completion of the year–end audit fieldwork, \nyou are meeting Zeeshan Bashir, Braking’s financial controller, to discuss outstanding matters from \nthe audit and any subsequent events. \nBraking's business is repairing aircraft wheels and brakes. Each time an aircraft lands it causes wear \nand tear to the wheels and brakes which Braking will repair. The company generates the majority of its \nrevenue through one–off repairs and an aircraft fleet repair contract paid on a regular basis throughout \nthe year. Braking is a subsidiary company within a large group that operates from a number of sites \nthroughout the UK. \nYou have a number of matters to discuss with Zeeshan, the first of which is a list of outstanding points \nnoted by the audit team which your audit senior has provided (Appendix 1). These points will require to \nbe cleared before the audit can be finalised and the audit opinion issued. Your audit partner, Stuart \nMiller, has asked you to evaluate each of the outstanding points and recommend what further audit \nprocedures, if any, will need to be performed to address these points. \nThe remaining items on the agenda for discussion with Zeeshan are the list of subsequent events he \nhas identified which have occurred since the year end which he believes may have an impact on the \naudit opinion. This morning, Stuart reviewed the list and has annotated his comments at the bottom of \neach point (Appendix 2). \nStuart has asked you to evaluate each of the subsequent events considering what, if any, \nadditional audit procedures would be required. He has also requested that, for each individual event, \nyou analyse the impact, if any, on the audit opinion in the event that the further audit procedures do \nnot resolve the issue satisfactorily. He also wishes you to recommend the appropriate type of audit \nreport to be issued should this be the case. Stuart has advised you that the appropriate materiality \nthreshold for all of the issues under consideration is £125,000. \nRequired: \n1. Evaluate each of the outstanding points from the audit, analysing why each is an issue and \nrecommend what further audit procedures, if any, will be required. \n(22 marks) \n2. For each of the subsequent events identified by Zeeshan: \na) evaluate each event, concluding whether each would be an adjusting or non–adjusting event \n(6 marks) \nb) consider what, if any, additional audit procedures would be required \n(8 marks) \nc) analyse the impact on the audit report and opinion, in the event that the further audit \nprocedures do not resolve the issue satisfactorily. \n(12 marks) \n(48 marks)\nAPPENDIX 1 (QUESTION 8) – Outstanding points from audit \nThese are the outstanding points from the audit of Braking. \nBank and Cash \n• \nThe bank confirmation letter received during the audit did not include a balance for \nBraking’s high interest investment account. The balance per the financial statements for \nthis account was £250,000. \nTrade receivables \n• \nA receivable due from Scotlandfly Ltd at the year end remained outstanding at the end of \nthe audit fieldwork visit. The cash had been promised and the outstanding balance was, \ntherefore, not provided for in the allowance for doubtful debt. The debt accounts for 23% \nof all outstanding trade receivables of £1,647,880 at 31 March 20X1. \nOther matters \n• \nThe previous operations director left the company at the beginning of the year and was \nnot replaced, with the finance director taking on a dual role. No confirmation has yet been \nreceived regarding the resignation of the director, and no disclosure has currently been \nmade in the financial statements regarding the resignation or termination payments.\nAPPENDIX 2 (QUESTION 8) – List of Subsequent Events provided \nby Zeeshan Bashir \n1. Liverpool building – Impairment \nSubsequent Event \nBraking owns a building in Liverpool which is rented out and classified as an investment property. In \nSeptember 20X0 the company obtained a third–party valuation for the land and buildings of £475,000 \nand is carrying the land and buildings at this value at 31 March 20X1. On 1 June 20X1, Braking \nreceived an offer of £175,000 from a building contractor looking to convert the property into a block of \nflats. This was considerably lower than the most recent valuation due to new planning restrictions \nimposed by the local council in March 20X1. \nThe sale has been approved by the board per the board minutes, but is not expected to occur until \nmid–October 20X1. \nClient Comment \nAs the offer to buy the land and buildings was made in the current financial year, I don’t believe the \nimpairment in the value of the land and buildings should be reflected at this stage as the sale has not \nbeen finalised. The property accounts for 9% of the overall net book value of property plant and \nequipment  held by the company. \nAudit Partner’s Comment \nAn impairment should be recognised as the regulations changed before the year end and therefore \nare relevant to the valuation at 31 March 20X1.  \n2. Outcome of legal case – contingent gain \nSubsequent Event \nBraking shares a rented hangar at Manchester Airport where it carries out repairs for one of its largest \ncustomers, Alba Airways Ltd (‘Airways’). The hangar is shared with a local airline, Fly– cheaply Limited \n(‘Flycheaply’). In December 20X0 a large fire broke out in one of Fly–cheaply’s planes awaiting service \nin the hangar. This caused significant damage to the interior of the hangar which had to be closed for \nrepair for two months. Braking subsequently sued Fly–cheaply for loss of income due to its inability to \nprovide repair services to Airways during this period. \nThe lost income Braking has claimed for is approximately £450,000. Fly–cheaply has contested the \nfact that they are liable to Braking as an investigation by the fire authorities found that the fire had \nbeen started deliberately by vandals. Braking believes, based on the advice of its legal advisors, that \nthis should not have any bearing on their claim and the compensation should be recognised in the \nfinancial statements to 31 March 20X1. \nThe court case is going to be finalised on 11 October 20X1 (after the accounts are due to be approved \nby the board) and we will know, for sure, if the revenue will be paid to Braking. \nClient Comment \nOur lawyers are advising us that we are very likely to win the case and the amount will be receivable \nfrom Fly–cheaply. We, therefore, want to adjust the financial statements to 31 March 20X1 to reflect \nthe compensation that will be paid relating to that year. \nAudit Partner’s Comment \nThe requirement under IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets is that \ncontingent assets should not be recognised, but if probable should be disclosed in the notes to the \naccounts. The realisation of income must be ‘virtually certain’ and only then should it be recognised as \nincome.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 8 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 25</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-08-case-p025.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-08-case source PDF page 25\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 26</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-08-case-p026.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-08-case source PDF page 26\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 27</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-08-case-p027.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-08-case source PDF page 27\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [25, 26, 27], "solution": [21, 22, 23, 24]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-10"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-009", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module9", "modules": ["Module9"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 9", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 17, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 28</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-09-q-p028.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-09-q source PDF page 28\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 25</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-09-a-p025.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-09-a source PDF page 25\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 26</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-09-a-p026.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-09-a source PDF page 26\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 9 (17 marks) \nIt is January 20X9. You are the audit senior in charge of Turbolite Ltd (‘Turbo’) for the year ended 30 \nSeptember 20X8. The audit fieldwork was completed early November and the file has been reviewed \nand you are at the stage of confirming any events after the reporting period prior to final completion. \nThe final draft of the financial statements is being prepared and you will receive them for review later \nthis week. The financial statements are due to be signed off at a board meeting scheduled for next \nweek. \nTurbo manufactures and assembles turbines, built to order as per customer specifications. Turbo is a \nfamily–owned company, having been established by two brothers near Coventry, England. The \nbusiness has grown over the course of the last 15 years via both organic and acquisitional means. \nTurbo manufactures turbines to any required scale, from small engines to those required for \nwindfarms, but always manufactures to order with no generic turbines being held in inventory. Turbo \nhas gained some significant contracts over the last few years for the larger turbines and the business \ncontinues to grow annually. \nYou have reviewed the audit file and extracted matters outstanding (Appendix). Your senior manager \nwould like you to consider any potential adjustments arising from the outstanding matters. Therefore, \nyour senior manager would like you to draft a summary of audit misstatements (‘SAM’) with \nconclusions justifying which items require adjustment. \nRequired: \nPrepare a SAM, explaining for each of the matters noted in the Appendix what misstatements, if any, \nshould be recorded and conclude on which items require to be corrected in order to allow an \nunmodified audit opinion. \n(17 marks)\nAPPENDIX (Question 9) – Matters outstanding from audit fieldwork \nThe draft financial statements for the year ended 30 September 20X8 show revenue of £22.1 million \nand total assets of £1.4 million. Materiality has been set at £120,000. \n1. On 6 October 20X8, the company received an invoice for plant and equipment costing £3.5 million \nwith an estimated useful life of five years which was installed and brought into use on 1 \nSeptember 20X8. The invoice was dated 5 October 20X8 and had been posted to the ledger \naccounts as an October 20X8 transaction and is not included in the financial statements for the \nyear ended 30 September 20X8. Turbine’s depreciation policy is to charge no depreciation in the \nyear of purchase and a full year in the year of disposal. No accounting entries have currently been \nmade for this. \n2. On 12 November 20X8, a batch of turbines which had been rejected by a customer were sold to \nanother customer, unamended, for £450,000. The turbines are currently included in finished goods \nin the financial statements for the year ended 30 September 20X8 at a cost of £500,000. \nManagement does not believe that an adjustment is required, as the rejection took place after year \nend. \n3. On 25 November 20X8, the company was advised by a tax specialist that because of the \ncomplexity of the issues involved in the HMRC enquiry into the company's tax affairs, which was \nlaunched in September 20X8, it is not possible to forecast the outcome of that enquiry. However, \nthe specialist has advised on the possible range of outcomes in respect of the additional tax \nliabilities ranges between a £10,000 and a £300,000 liability. There is currently no reference to this \nmatter in the draft financial statements. Turbo would be able to meet the cash liability of any \noutcome, due to sufficient cash reserves. \n4. On 9 November 20X8, Turbo received notice that one of their customers, Drivetrain Ltd, had gone \ninto liquidation. As at 30 September 20X8, there was a balance on the trade receivables ledger \nshowing £145,000 owed from Drivetrain Ltd. No payments were received after the year end, and \nthe amount owed was overdue as at the year end. Management have updated the financial \nstatements to reflect this liquidation. Drivetrain only comprises approximately 3% of annual \nturnover, so there is no potential going concern impact for Turbo as a result of these events.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 9 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 28</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-09-case-p028.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-09-case source PDF page 28\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 29</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-09-case-p029.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-09-case source PDF page 29\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [28, 29], "solution": [25, 26]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-9"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-010", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module9", "modules": ["Module9"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 10", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 45, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 30</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-10-q-p030.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-10-q source PDF page 30\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 27</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-10-a-p027.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-10-a source PDF page 27\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 28</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-10-a-p028.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-10-a source PDF page 28\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 29</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-10-a-p029.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-10-a source PDF page 29\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 10 (45 marks) \nYou are the audit senior of client, Sailing Limited (‘Sailing’). Sailing is a manufacturer that sells outdoor \nclothing and equipment, specialising in fishing, sailing and other water sports, to a number of online \nretailers. The year–end of Sailing is 31 May 20X2. \nYou are working on the trade receivables section of the audit and have already sent out the trade \nreceivables circularisation as at 30 April 20X2 as part of the audit work. You had already documented \nthe results of several confirmation responses received (Appendix 1) but have since received several \nmore responses (Appendix 2). \nAs part of the audit programme for trade receivables testing, you are still required to complete the \ndocumentation of the new responses received. Once documented, you are required to design \nprocedures over any balances not yet agreed as well as any further procedures required to complete \nthe trade payables circularisation testing. \nRequired: \n1. Complete the trade receivable circularisation workpaper for the new responses included in \nAppendix 2, documenting the reasons for any differences identified; \n(10 marks) \n2. Design procedures over those balances not yet agreed, based on the information provided in \nAppendix 1 and any findings from Requirement 1; and \n(30 marks) \n3. Design any other relevant substantive procedures required to complete the trade receivable \ncircularisation. \n(5 marks) \n(45 marks)\nAPPENDIX 1 (QUESTION 10) – Trade receivables circularisation \naudit workpaper \nCustomer \nBalance per trade \nreceivables ledger \nBalance per trade\nreceivables \ncircularisation Reason for difference \n \n£ \n£  \nAirstream Limited \n136,402.78 \n \nBoom Limited \n245,891.45 \n245,891.45 Agreed balance \nCirus Limited \n3,678.00 \n \nDavits Limited \n42,378.43 \n \nFlying Fish Limited \n20,054.99 \n \nGalley Limited \n15,285.46 \n10,784.19 An invoice for £4,501.27 was \nreceived by Galley Limited on 3 May 20X2 \nHalyard Limited \n98,682.88 \n98,682.88 Agreed balance \nIAS Limited \n145,895.65 \n \nJumpstart Limited \n25,478.98 \n36,694.87 Balance confirmed by client \nas at 14 May 20X2 \nKeel Limited \n26,895.25 \nAdministrator replied – no expected \nrecovery of balance or goods \nLifeline Limited \n402,111.89 \n \nM Limited \n15,117.81 \n15,117.81 Agreed balance\nAPPENDIX 2 (QUESTION 10) – Additional trade receivable \ncircularisation responses received\nDear Auditor, \nWe confirm that according to our records a balance of £235,945.00 was owed by \nus to Sailing Ltd at 30 April 20X2. \nName of company: Lifeline Ltd \nItems making up difference: bank transfer made to Sailing Limited of £166,166.89 \non 30 April 20X2 re Invoice LL478 \nFurther explanation if \napplicable: Signed: L Belt \nPosition: Financial Assistant \nAPPENDIX 2 (QUESTION 10) (Continued)", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 10 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 30</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-10-case-p030.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-10-case source PDF page 30\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 31</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-10-case-p031.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-10-case source PDF page 31\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 32</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-10-case-p032.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-10-case source PDF page 32\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 33</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-10-case-p033.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-10-case source PDF page 33\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [30, 31, 32, 33], "solution": [27, 28, 29]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-9"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-011", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module12", "modules": ["Module3", "Module5", "Module12"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 11", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 44, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 35</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-11-q-p035.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-11-q source PDF page 35\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 30</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-11-a-p030.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-11-a source PDF page 30\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 31</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-11-a-p031.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-11-a source PDF page 31\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 32</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-11-a-p032.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-11-a source PDF page 32\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 33</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-11-a-p033.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-11-a source PDF page 33\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "QUESTION 11 (44 marks) \nIt is August 20X1 and you are an assurance practitioner in McFly Assurance (‘McFly’), a Scottish \nbased accountancy and audit firm. McFly’s external audit and other assurance teams work \nindependently of each other. Nibbles Snacks Limited (‘Nibbles’), a current audit client, has approached \nthe firm regarding a potential additional assurance assignment. \nNibbles is a food production company operating from a large factory site on the west coast of \nScotland. It prides itself in the use of local sustainable produce, and its low impact production facility \nwhich aids in maintaining sustainability in the local countryside. This is their main differentiator from \nother companies in the same industry and a key contributor to the significant market share that they \nenjoy. \nAs a food production company Nibbles must operate under the terms of an environmental licence \nissued by the Scottish Environmental Protection Agency (SEPA). This licence is termed a PPC licence \nand is issued under the PPC regime governed by the Pollution Prevention and Control (Scotland) \nRegulations 2012. \nPPC licences are designed to control activities that could lead to pollution or environmental damage. \nSEPA performs compliance activities to check whether licence holders are meeting the terms of their \nlicences. This Compliance Assessment Scheme (CAS) enables SEPA to assess and categorise the \ncompliance of the licence holder. \nIn early 20X0 Nibbles had a succession of pollution incidents, which combined with poor site \nmanagement contributed to SEPA issuing a ‘Very Poor’ CAS score in its annual assessment in June \n20X1. These pollution incidents were included in complaints to SEPA from local residents regarding \nliquid pollutant discharges to a local river killing wild salmon stocks. \nNibbles worked with SEPA and an action plan with clear requirements was proposed. This included \nthe preparation and delivery of a site improvement plan, including documentation of increased and \nappropriate staff training, and proactive monitoring of all liquid discharges to ensure they are within the \nrequired limits as set by SEPA. Nibbles is required to submit a report to SEPA demonstrating its \ncurrent compliance with the agreed action plan. This report will provide a point of reference for SEPA \nin its next Compliance Assessment in November 20X1. \nThe small internal audit team at Nibbles has been key in the documentation and implementation of the \nSEPA action plan and the Head of Internal Audit is due to commence extended paternity leave in two \nweeks. \nIn your capacity as an assurance practitioner, Nibbles’ board of directors (‘the board’) has asked you \nto review the corrective action plan and improvement to controls that Nibbles has put in place, and to \ngive assurance that these are functioning correctly and achieving the required outcome. Nibbles has \nalso been approached by a local TV station to participate in a documentary into the pollution in the \nlocal river and management intends to highlight the action plan and the assurance obtained from \nMcFly to help assure the public that it is undertaking appropriate actions to address the failings. \nMcFly has previously undertaken an additional assurance engagement for Nibbles relating to an \napplication for a Scottish Government Capital Grant in addition to the annual external audit. As a \nresult, it was clear that not all of Nibble’s directors were aware of the company’s business targets and \nthe commerciality of its underlying activities. Furthermore, it was clear that the board was not always in \nagreement on their long–term business goals and the need to remedy deficiencies incontrols. For \nexample, there were various objections to recommendations regarding improvements in control \nactivities within the audit team’s management letter. \nFollowing a review of all the information provided, you have confirmed that this work fulfils the \nnecessary elements to be classed as an assurance engagement, but have still to evaluate which type \nof assurance engagement would be suitable and the relevant risks associated with accepting this \nengagement. The board has agreed a flexible amount in its annual budget as a fee for this \nengagement, dependent on the work required.\nYou have also identified that due to a lack of expertise in your firm regarding assessment of pollution \nlevels, it will be necessary to engage an external expert to ensure the actual levels are as reported to \nSEPA. The engagement team will place reliance on the work of the expert and the assurance report \nwill be sent to SEPA to provide assurance of Nibbles compliance. \nFollowing a recommendation from one of your colleagues you have approached a Scottish based \nconsultant, McStag Environmental Services LLP (‘McStag’), which should be able to provide the \nexpertise required for this engagement. McStag is based in Inverness, but operates worldwide and \nhas a wide range of inhouse environmental consultancy services, each employing specialists in their \nown field. McStag’s employees have a wide diversity of degrees and experience and are affiliated to \ntheir own specialised professional institutes. \nOne of McStag’s partners, Dr Joe Trout, is being proposed to undertake the work on behalf of McStag. \nHe has a degree in Marine Science and 10 years post–doctorate expertise. Following a brief \ntelephone chat, he is keen to undertake the assignment as he is familiar with Nibbles and would be \nhappy to stay with his aunt and his cousin who live nearby and work at Nibble’s production facility. The \ntechnical language used to explain his roles and responsibilities within McStag and relevance to the \nengagement was outside your comfort zone and your team have been unable to help in interpreting \nthis. \nAn evaluation of McStag is required to be completed in order to confirm whether reliance can be \nplaced on the organisation for the purposes of the assurance engagement. \nRequired: \n1. Explain, with examples of relevant opinions, the two types of assurance engagement that could be \nundertaken for the proposed Nibbles engagement, concluding which would be the most suitable. \n(11 marks) \n2.  \na) Evaluate the acceptance risks to your firm from accepting this assurance engagement. \n(12 marks) \nb) Recommend suitable actions or safeguards to put in place to mitigate the risks identified in 2(a). \n(8 marks) \n3. Evaluate the proposed external expert to confirm whether it is likely that reliance can be placed on \nhis work and identify any further evidence required in order to form a conclusion. \n(13 marks) \n(44 marks)", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 11 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 34</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-11-case-p034.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-11-case source PDF page 34\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 35</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-11-case-p035.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-11-case source PDF page 35\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [34, 35], "solution": [30, 31, 32, 33]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-3", "aa-module-5", "aa-module-12"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-012", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module11", "modules": ["Module4", "Module11"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 12", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 10, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 36</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-12-q-p036.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-12-q source PDF page 36\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 34</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-12-a-p034.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-12-a source PDF page 34\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 12 (10 marks) \nYou are the audit manager planning the audit of the group financial statements of SJK Group Ltd \n(‘SJK’ or ‘the group’) for the year ending 31 October 20X3. This is the first year that your firm is \nauditing the group. SJK is a non–trading holding company that owns 100% of the share capital in its \nthree trading subsidiaries: \n• \nNessDhu Whisky Limited (‘NessDhu’) – producer of NessDhu whisky \n• \nSoups and Sauces Limited (‘Soups’) – manufacturer of high–quality soups and sauces \n• \nLowland Garden Centres Limited (‘Lowland’) – retailer of plants and associated items from \nthree sites in Northern Ireland \nAcceptance procedures for the group have been completed. Ahead of the year end, your audit partner, \nAmelia Watkins, has asked you to recommend a level for overall group materiality and trivial threshold, \nusing the extracts from the consolidated management accounts in Appendix 1, explaining your \nreasons. \nRequired: \nRecommend a level for overall group materiality and trivial threshold, explaining your reasons. \n(10 marks)\nAPPENDIX 1 (Question 12) – Extract from consolidated \nmanagement accounts \nExtracts from consolidated management accounts \n \n9 months to 31 \nYear ended 31\nOctober 20X3\nYear ended 31\nOctober 20X2\nJuly 20X3\n \nActual\nBudget\nActual\n \n£000\n£000\n£000\nRevenue \n25,548\n34,832\n33,688\nGross profit \n10,705\n14,978\n14,220\nProfit before income taxes \n4,854\n7,315\n6,801\nProfit after taxation \n3,932\n5,925\n5,482\nTotal assets \n84,354\n85,012\n70,633\nNet assets \n75,690\n74,895\n65,445", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 12 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 36</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-12-case-p036.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-12-case source PDF page 36\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 37</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-12-case-p037.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-12-case source PDF page 37\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [36, 37], "solution": [34]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-4", "aa-module-11"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-013", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module9", "modules": ["Module4", "Module8", "Module9"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 13", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 48, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 38</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-13-q-p038.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-13-q source PDF page 38\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 39</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-13-q-p039.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-13-q source PDF page 39\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 35</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-13-a-p035.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-13-a source PDF page 35\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 36</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-13-a-p036.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-13-a source PDF page 36\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 37</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-13-a-p037.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-13-a source PDF page 37\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 13 (48 marks) \nYou are an audit manager at Clairs Advisory Group LLP (‘Clairs’) and are working on the external \naudit of the financial statements of Lovely Drinks Company Ltd (‘Lovely Drinks’) for the year ended 31 \nDecember 20X3. Lovely Drinks has been a client of your firm for several years and this is your first \nyear as audit manager. You are in the process of planning the audit and you are considering the work \nto be performed around inventory and trade receivables. \nLovely Drinks was founded 35 years ago and manufactures and sells a variety of fruity soft drinks to \ndrinks suppliers and retailers. Two of the largest UK supermarket chains, Birch Retailers plc and \nBarnbury Food Group plc are its biggest customers. In the previous year to 31 December 20X2 the \ncompany’s turnover was £10.2m and it is expected that performance in 20X3 will be similar. \nThe company is based in the town of Kings Lynn, England, with a head office building adjoining two \nlarge warehouses. Warehouse one houses the production equipment and raw materials to make the \ndrinks and warehouse two stores finished goods ready for dispatch. The types of inventory held are as \nfollows: \nRaw materials (‘RM’) • Bulk purchases of liquids and powders used to manufacture drinks. \n \n• Packaging bottles and boxes for the drinks. \nFinished goods (‘FG’) • Drinks ready for sale. \nInventory has been identified as a material balance within the financial statements. As part of the audit \nplanning process, your audit team has been provided with the interim inventory listing, per Lovely \nDrinks’s general ledger, as at 30 September 20X3, which has been analysed using your firm’s audit \ndata analytics software (Appendix 1). The results of the analysis include information on the usage of \nraw materials during the production process as well as sales data related to the finished goods. \nYou have also received some commentary from Lovely Drinks’s finance manager, Tanya Lorenz, \n(Appendix 2) concerning inventory production and performance during the nine–month period to 30 \nSeptember 20X3. \nYour audit partner, Penny Rowe, has asked you to evaluate the results of the audit data analytics \nexercise over inventory, analysing any identified areas of audit risk. She would also like you to design \nany relevant substantive procedures required in response to the risks identified. \nPenny also highlighted that she wished to undertake a trade receivables circularisation exercise using \na sample of receivables balances. She has requested that a sample is selected from balances as at \n30 September 20X3 with relevant roll forward procedures performed for the subsequent period from 1 \nOctober 20X3 to 31 December 20X3. Penny has provided you with an extract from the trade \nreceivables ledger as at 30 September 20X3 as well as details relating to the customer balances noted \nduring the audit planning meeting with Lovely Drinks’s finance director (Appendix 3). Penny would like \nyou select which balances should be sampled for the exercise, analysing your rationale. Your focus \nshould be on balances where there is evidence of risk of misstatement. \nRequired: \n1.  \na) Evaluate the results of the audit data analytics exercise over inventory, analysing any identified \nareas of audit risk; and \n(17 marks) \nb) Design relevant substantive procedures in response to the risks identified in requirement 1(a) \n(16 marks)\n2. Using the information in Appendix 3, identify which balances from the trade receivables ledger \nshould be selected for the trade receivables circularisation exercise, analysing your rationale. \n(Note: you do not need to explain your rationale for those balances not selected). \n(15 marks) \n(48 marks)\nAPPENDIX 1 (QUESTION 13) – Extract from the report on the data \nanalytic procedures performed over inventory at 30 September \n20X3 provided by Clairs audit data analytics team \nA -  Objectives of audit data analytic (‘ADA’) routine – planning analytics inventory \nThe objective of the ADA is to identify any potential material misstatements within inventory. \nThe following routines have been run as part of the audit data analytic tool: \n1) Identify any items where cost exceeds net realisable value (‘NRV’) \n2) Identify any perishable items which are past their expiry date \n3) Identify any slow–moving items – this is determined as any finished good where the \n4) ‘last sale’ was before 1 April 20X3 \n5) Identify any duplicate inventory items \n6) Identify any items in breach of first in first out (‘FIFO’) policy \n7) Identify any items which have a short shelf life – this is determined by the raw \n8) material ‘expiry date’ being within one month of the ‘last receipt in warehouse’ \nAny inventory items identified as a result of the above routines are flagged by the relevant routine \nnumber. \nB -  Cleansing of data \nData was extracted from the Lovely Drinks system by the Clairs audit data analytics team using their \ndata extraction tool and it did not require cleansing. The data outputs have been split into raw \nmaterials and finished goods to be consistent with the inventory listing. \nC -  Completeness and integrity check of input data \nThe data has been checked for accuracy, completeness, validity and reliability by the audit team. This \nincluded checking the overall NRV, total number of inventory items and the inventory provision \nincluded in the 30 September 20X3 management accounts.\nAPPENDIX 1 (QUESTION 13) (continued) \nD -  Results \nRaw materials \nFlag ID \nDetail \nExpiry date \nLast receipt in \nwarehouse \nLast used during \nproduction \nCost \nBudget usage \nfull year \nActual usage YTD \n2 \nCH–52 \nCherry concentrate \n31/12/20X2 \n10/12/20X0 \n05/03/20X1 \n£1,540 10,000 bottles  \n4 \nGP–420 Grape concentrate \n05/02/20X4 \n10/02/20X3 \n26/06/20X3 \n£5,635 75,000 bottles 45,000 bottles \n4, 5 \nGP– \n420o \nOrganic grape concentrate 30/07/20X4 \n05/06/20X3 \n18/09/20X3 \n£6,480 – \n20,000 bottles \n6 \nFA–64 \nFresh apple juice \n10/10/20X3 \n20/09/20X3 \n02/08/20X3 \n£3,766 750 bottles \n760 bottles \n \nFinished goods \nFlag Product name \nLast manufacture date Expiry date Last sale \nCost \nSelling price Budgeted sales full year Actual sales YTD \n1 \nTropical \n15/08/20X3 \n30/01/20X4 30/08/20X3 £2,322 £2,150 \n20,000 bottles \n17,000 bottles \n3 \nPineapple and lime 04/07/20X3 \n16/12/20X3 25/03/20X3 £3,510 £5,250 \n3,000 bottles \n2,000 bottles\nAPPENDIX 2 (QUESTION 13) – Commentary from Lovely Drinks’s \nfinance manager, Tanya Lorenz, concerning inventory production \nand performance during the nine–month period to 30 September \n20X3 \nProduction process \nEach day a different drink is manufactured by the company. The drink–making equipment uses \ncomputerised recipe cards to determine what ingredients to mix at each part of the process and the \nvolumes required. Before the manufacture of a specific drink begins, a production assistant selects \nthe correct recipe card on the computer and puts the various ingredients into the machines. These are \nreviewed by a production manager before the process begins. \nThe production team use a first in first out (‘FIFO’) policy to ensure that the raw materials that \nhave the earliest expiry date are used first. \nThe machines are cleaned at the end of the day. As such there is no WIP at the end of the production \nday. \nOther comments \nThe grape concentrate (ID:GP–420) has been replaced with an organic grape concentrate by the \nsupplier. The product is exactly the same apart from being re–branded organic. In October 20X3 the \nID for the organic product was updated to GP–420o and the recipe card was updated to ensure that \nthis item is used for all future production. \nPineapple and lime juice was specifically made for one customer. Following poor sales, the customer \nhas decided to discontinue stocking this product within its stores.\nAPPENDIX 3 (QUESTION 13) – Trade receivables extract as at 30 \nSeptember 20X3 \nCustomer \nBalance per \nsales \nledger\nOverall \ntotal sales \nyear to date\nPrior year\ntotal sales\nAged analysis in days \n0–30 \n31–60\n61–90 \n90+\nBirch Retailers plc \n£6,940\n£870,888 £1,138,111\n£3,820 \n£3,120\n– \n–\nFresh Fruits Deluxe Ltd \n£27,543\n£163,873\n£217,529\n£2,875 £13,950\n£5,169 \n£5,549\nMartin’s Vegan Store \n£540\n£1,600\n£540 \n–\n– \n–\nTasty Veg Ltd \n£2,012\n£12,091\n£18,900\n£1,659 \n£353\n– \n–\nMayburn Farm Shop \n(£1,590)\n£48,099\n£38,748\n£1,304 (£2,894)\n \nJuice Box Ltd \n£215\n£590\n£700\n£205 \n–\n– \n£10\nBarnbury Food Group plc \n£75,980\n£785,928 £1,298,593\n£16,905 £25,012\n£23,976 £10,087\nNotes from the audit planning meeting with Lovely Drinks’s finance director \n• \nInvoices are issued on the same day as the goods despatched note and Lovely Drinks gives \nall customers credit terms of 60 days to pay from date of invoice. \n• \nFresh Fruits Deluxe Ltd has been a customer for many years but recently it has been in the \nnews for being in financial difficulty. The finance director highlighted that Lovely drinks’s \nreceivables account manager has been in contact with the customer about late payments. \nHowever, it is yet to receive a reply. \n• \nMartin’s Vegan Store is a new customer in 20X3, located near to the warehouses. The \ncustomer has been making frequent small orders and has always settled its account within a \nweek of invoicing. \n• \nAs well as purchasing juice from Lovely Drinks, Mayburn Farm Shop also supplies fresh fruit \nto Lovely Drinks and both the sales and purchases are handled through the one account on \nthe system. When a purchase is made, it offsets this against any sales invoices on the \nsystem. \n• \nBarnbury Food Group are currently on a payment plan after the customer struggled to pay its \ninvoices. As a result, the customer has invoices outstanding past the normal credit term. \nLovely Drinks’s management have agreed they are comfortable with this as Barnbury Food \nGroup has recently secured new investment.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 13 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 38</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-13-case-p038.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-13-case source PDF page 38\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 39</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-13-case-p039.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-13-case source PDF page 39\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 40</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-13-case-p040.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-13-case source PDF page 40\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 41</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-13-case-p041.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-13-case source PDF page 41\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 42</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-13-case-p042.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-13-case source PDF page 42\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 43</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-13-case-p043.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-13-case source PDF page 43\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [38, 39, 40, 41, 42, 43], "solution": [35, 36, 37]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-4", "aa-module-8", "aa-module-9"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-014", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module6", "modules": ["Module6"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 14", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 45, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 44</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-14-q-p044.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-14-q source PDF page 44\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 38</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-14-a-p038.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-14-a source PDF page 38\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 39</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-14-a-p039.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-14-a source PDF page 39\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 40</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-14-a-p040.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-14-a source PDF page 40\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 14 (45 marks) \nYou are the audit manager responsible for the external audit of the financial statements of Pharmalux \nLimited (‘Pharmalux’) for the year ending 31 October 20X0. Pharmalux has been an audit client of your \nfirm, Smith, Allan and Cooper LLP (‘SA&C’), a firm of Chartered Accountants and Registered Auditors, for \nseveral years. You are currently planning the audit approach to the audit of Pharmalux’s inventory. \nPharmalux was founded 9 years ago and specialises in researching, developing and selling a range \nof homeopathic alternatives to conventional medicines. Pharmalux’s head office, manufacturing \nfacility and adjoining central warehouse are based in Inverness, Scotland. This facility holds all of the \nraw materials for the production of the homeopathic medicines (‘medicines’) and is also home to the \nresearch and development centre, which focuses upon the development and testing of new medicines. \nPharmalux also owns a number of finished goods warehouses and distribution facilities throughout the \nworld, which enable Pharmalux to distribute its medicines to its worldwide customer base. \nInventory has been identified as a material balance within the financial statements. During the prior year \naudit, the audit team reported a number of weaknesses around the inventory management process in \nthe warehouse in Inverness including controls over the appropriateness of its inventory provision. As a \nresult, Pharmalux has reviewed its inventory processes across all locations and made a number of \nchanges during the current financial year. The audit team plans to place reliance on controls within the \nrevised system. \nThe audit partner, Joanne Dixon, has asked you to identify the key controls within the Pharmalux inventory \nsystem in Inverness which are designed effectively to prevent, detect or correct misstatements within \nthe inventory balance and to evaluate why each control would be classified as a key control for the purpose \nof the external audit. She would also like you to design test of controls to confirm the operating \neffectiveness of each key control. \nThe revised inventory system has been documented by a member of your audit team, Peter Daniels, \nfollowing discussions with the Pharmalux inventory accountant (Appendix). Joanne has requested that \nyou do not consider the full annual inventory count performed at year end by Pharmalux as part of this \nevaluation, as it is already planned that your firm will look to attend year end inventory counts at some of the \ncompany’s locations. You are also not required to consider the valuation of inventory and general \nprovisioning, as this will be covered by another team member. \nRequired: \na)  \ni. \nIdentify the key controls within the inventory system, at the Inverness site, which are designed \nto effectively prevent, detect and correct misstatements in the inventory balance; and \n(8 marks) \nii. \n(ii) Evaluate why each control would be classified as a key control for the purpose of the \nexternal audit \n(16 marks) \nb) Design test of controls to confirm the operating effectiveness of each identified key control. \n(16 marks) \nc) Describe how this audit approach may change depending on the results of controls testing. \n(5 marks) \n(45 marks)\nAPPENDIX – Notes of Pharmalux inventory system prepared by \nPeter Daniels, audit senior \nTypes of inventory held: \nRaw materials – Bulk purchases of liquids and powders used to manufacture medicines and carton \nboxes for the packaging of medicines for sale. \nWork in progress (‘WIP’) – Medicines currently within the manufacturing process. Due to the speed of \nthe manufacturing process, there is never any WIP at the end of the production day. \nFinished goods, medicines ready for sale – These are medicines sold directly to pharmacies and health \nfood stores or direct to consumers through the Pharmalux website. \nGeneral \nThere are a number of warehouse assistants (‘assistants’) employed at each location, with a \nwarehouse manager in each warehouse and distribution centre. As with all Pharmalux staff, \nassistants must sign an annual ‘self–declaration of good character’ that is maintained on their \npersonnel file by the Human Resources (‘HR’) department. \nReceipt of raw materials \nOn a daily basis, raw materials are received in the central warehouse, in Inverness, at a single \ngoods–in bay. On receipt, an assistant scans the raw materials via the barcode on the container with \na handheld scanner that matches the physical delivery received to the purchase order (‘PO’) within \nthe Pharmalux enterprise resource planning (‘ERP’) system. The assistant also manually enters the \nexpiration date of the goods as stated on the container into the ERP system. If there are any \ndiscrepancies between the ordered and delivered quantities or the item is scanned more than once, \nthis will be shown by the system as an error and the goods cannot be checked in to inventory until it \nhas been resolved by the warehouse manager. Once the information is complete, a goods received \nnote (‘GRN’) is created and the ERP system will mark the line on the PO as ‘received pending quality \ncontrol’. \nThe raw materials are physically moved into the quality control (‘QC’) area of the warehouse which is \nsegregated from the goods–in and main storage locations. \nAt the QC area, an assistant will inspect the raw materials for any signs of damage, including \nconfirming that any tamper–proof seals are not broken and, if satisfactory, will change the status of \nthe GRN to ‘fully received’. This shows the goods as available for use within the manufacturing \nprocess. Once the goods have passed QC, they are reloaded onto the forklift truck and moved to their \nstorage location. Each storage location has an individual barcode that is scanned with the handheld \nscanner and updates the system with the location of the goods. \nManufacturing of finished goods \nMedicines are manufactured in sequentially numbered batches, each with a traceable date of \nmanufacture and expiration date. The production team is responsible for releasing a sequentially \nnumbered bill of materials (‘BOM’) for each batch of medicine to be manufactured as well as a \nmatching production order. The BOM contains details of the exact quantities of each of the raw \nmaterials that are to be delivered from the relevant storage locations to the manufacturing facility. No \nreview is carried out over the sequential numbering of batches or BOMs for completeness.\nAPPENDIX (continued) \nAssistants receive a notification on their handheld scanner when a new BOM is available. They \nretrieve the raw materials in the BOM from each storage location noted on the scanner and scan the \nlocation barcode and the item barcode to update the inventory system with the raw materials used \nwithin a particular batch of medicine. They then move the physical raw materials to the manufacturing \narea. In some cases, the assistants need to weigh a quantity of raw materials for use in the \nmanufacturing process and Pharmalux has large scales available within the warehouse for this \npurpose. Once the raw materials have been delivered to the manufacturing area, the assistant marks \nthe BOM as complete on their handheld scanner and this automatically updates the inventory records \ntransferring the cost of raw materials to work in progress. \nOn completion of the manufacturing process, the resulting finished goods are transferred to the \nfinished goods storage location. The production manager confirms the quantity of goods and signs off \nthe completed production order which results in the inventory system being updated to remove work \nin progress and the completed items being recorded as finished goods in the relevant finished goods \nstorage location. \nIssue of finished goods \nThe sales team releases a picking list generated within the ERP system from the customer order. The \nassistants receive a notification on their handheld scanner, which will state the batch number of the \nmedicine that should be picked. Medicines are despatched on a first–in–first–out basis. The \nassistants pick the finished goods from the storage location and scan the location and item barcodes. \nThis creates a goods despatch note (‘GDN’) in the ERP system that is marked as ‘issued pending \ncheck’. \nAn assistant will perform an independent check of the medicines prior to packing them for \ntransportation. This check involves agreeing the customer, address, medicines requested and \nquantity to the customer order and to the GDN. Once this check is performed, the assistant will mark \nthe GDN as ‘final issued’ on their handheld scanner. Every quarter, the warehouse manager will \nreview an exception report of any unfulfilled picking lists and GDNs during that period and will follow \nup on any issues within two working days. \nHolding of inventory \nAccess to the warehouse is controlled by electronic key cards that are programmed to only allow \naccess to authorised staff members. Within the warehouse, some finished medicines are segregated \nin a separate area with a further number keypad entry code required to access it. \nPharmalux holds raw material inventory that is owned by a third–party company A+ Pharmaceutical \nSupplies Limited and this is physically segregated from the inventory owned by Pharmalux. When \nitems of consignment inventory are received as goods–in, they are stored in a separate area and not \nrecorded within the company’s inventory records.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 14 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 44</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-14-case-p044.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-14-case source PDF page 44\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 45</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-14-case-p045.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-14-case source PDF page 45\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 46</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-14-case-p046.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-14-case source PDF page 46\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [44, 45, 46], "solution": [38, 39, 40]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-6"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-015", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module1", "modules": ["Module1"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 15", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 20, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 47</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-15-q-p047.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-15-q source PDF page 47\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 41</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-15-a-p041.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-15-a source PDF page 41\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 42</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-15-a-p042.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-15-a source PDF page 42\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 15 (20 marks) \nIt is October 20X3. You have been asked to manage the audit of Dalriada Dairy Products plc \n(‘Dalriada’) as the previous audit manager, who dealt with the audit for the last 15 years, has recently \nretired. The audit planning has been completed for the financial year to 31 December 20X3 and an \ninterim audit is about to commence. \nFrom the audit files, you have ascertained the following information: \n1. Dalriada achieved a listing on the London Stock Exchange (LSE) six years ago. It is involved in \nthe purchase of milk from the farming community, in the processing of the milk to produce \ncheese, cream, etc. and in the sale and distribution of the products to wholesalers. Dalriada has \nproduction facilities in six locations throughout the UK. \n2. Dalriada has been a client of the firm for over 20 years. It has been part of the same partner’s \naudit portfolio for the entire period. The financial director is the brother of the audit partner. \n3. The firm has recently received some information from a former transport manager at Dalriada, \nwho was made redundant. They have stated that the drivers have been instructed by one of the \ndirectors to alter the vehicle tachographs to circumvent the accurate recording of driving hours \nand speeds. You have some concerns over the motivation behind the supply of this information \nbut are aware that it ought to be considered as part of the audit process. \nIn light of this information, you decide to prepare a short note of the issues which concern you for \ndiscussion with the audit partner. \nRequired: \nPrepare information in which you: \na) Evaluate the specific risk factors which require addressing in relation to your firm’s continued \ninvolvement with the audit engagement; and \n(12 marks) \nb) Recommend any relevant safeguards or actions your firm should put in place to reduce these \nrisks. \n(8 marks) \n(20 marks)", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 15 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 47</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-15-case-p047.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-15-case source PDF page 47\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [47], "solution": [41, 42]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-1"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-016", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module10", "modules": ["Module10"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 16", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 29, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 48</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-16-q-p048.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-16-q source PDF page 48\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 43</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-16-a-p043.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-16-a source PDF page 43\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 44</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-16-a-p044.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-16-a source PDF page 44\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 16 (29 marks) \nYou are the audit partner for High Five Ltd (‘High Five’), which manufactures smart phones. The \ncompany has been experiencing significant trading difficulties caused by severe price competition in \nthe marketplace and high development expenditure incurred in connection with designing a new \ngeneration of phone. \nYour firm is currently auditing High Five’s financial statements for the year ended 30 June 20X4. \nMatters contained in the draft Audit Highlights Memorandum have made you question whether it is \nappropriate for the accounts to be prepared on a going concern basis. These matters are as follows: \n1. The company has recorded trading losses of £550,000 in the year, causing the company’s draft \nbalance sheet at 30 June 20X4 to show a deficit in the shareholders’ funds of £100,000; \n2. The company has a bank overdraft of £250,000 which is in excess of its agreed overdraft limit of \n£200,000. Correspondence with the bank indicates that as the overdraft is unsecured the bank is \nnot prepared to increase the facility and is insisting that the balance be brought within the agreed \nlimit by 31 October 20X4, the date on which the overdraft is due for renewal. A guarantee has \nbeen sought from the parent company to act as security for the bank on the renewal of the \noverdraft facility; \n3. High Five owes its parent company £150,000 for the supply of raw materials and components. \nSettlement of this amount is six months overdue. The parent company has been asked to provide \na letter confirming that it will not press for payment; and \n4. High Five’s budgets indicate that a loss of £100,000 was expected for the year to 30 June 20X4 \nfollowed by a healthy return to profit in the year to 30 June 20X5. The audit team has been told by \nmanagement that the budgets are going to be reappraised.  \nThe finance director has asked what the effect would be on the audit report and audit opinion if it \nis concluded that there is a material uncertainty in relation to going concern. \nRequired: \n1. Design further audit testing which you require to carry out to satisfy your firm in relation to going \nconcern; and \n(19 marks) \n2. Evaluate, if it is concluded that there is material uncertainty in relation to going concern, \ni. \nthe different circumstances that could result in a modified audit opinion or audit report; and \nii. \nthe subsequent modification to the audit opinion and/or audit report. \n(10 marks) \n(29 marks)", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 16 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 48</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-16-case-p048.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-16-case source PDF page 48\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [48], "solution": [43, 44]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-10"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-017", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module4", "modules": ["Module4"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 17", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 48, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 49</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-17-q-p049.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-17-q source PDF page 49\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 45</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-17-a-p045.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-17-a source PDF page 45\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 46</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-17-a-p046.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-17-a source PDF page 46\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 47</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-17-a-p047.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-17-a source PDF page 47\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 48</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-17-a-p048.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-17-a source PDF page 48\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 17 (48 marks) \nIt is December 20X8 and you are the audit manager on the external audit of the financial statements \nof Hobson Mowers Limited (‘Hobson’) for the year ending 31 January 20X9. Hobson has been a client \nof your firm for several years and this is your first year as audit manager. You are currently preparing \nsections of the audit strategy memorandum. Hobson was established 30 years ago and is a \nsuccessful supplier of grass cutting machinery to the domestic and commercial market across \nScotland, representing a number of leading brands. In recent years, Hobson has grown from one \nshowroom in the Scottish Borders to operating from seven locations throughout Scotland and one in \nNorth England. The most recent expansion was the development during 20X8 of a new showroom \nnear Alnwick, Northumberland.  \nEach location has a showroom, a spare parts department and a fully equipped workshop. The \ncompany operates three divisions: domestic, commercial and parts and servicing. More information \non each division and other relevant information can be found in the points forward schedule from the \nprior year audit file (Appendix 1).  \nAs part of the audit planning process, the audit engagement partner, Joanna Scott, met with the \ncompany’s finance director Jim Rae. At this meeting, Jim provided a copy of the most recent \nmanagement accounts for the 10 months ending 30 November 20X8, which have been analysed by \nthe audit data analytics department of your firm and their audit data analytic software (Appendix 2). A \nnumber of other matters were also discussed and noted by Joanna (Appendix 3).  \nFollowing the planning meeting, Joanna has asked you to prepare sections to be included in the audit \nstrategy memorandum, including an evaluation of the results of the audit data analytic exercise for the \npurpose of the planning analytical review, and an evaluation of any additional risks identified from \nyour review of the information gathered during the planning process. However, she does not want you \nto outline the audit approach to the risks at this stage as these will be discussed once she has had an \nopportunity to review your work.  \nRequired:  \n1. Evaluate the results of the audit data analytic exercise for the purpose of the planning analytical \nreview  \n(30 marks)  \n2. Evaluate any additional audit risks identified from review of the information gathered  \n(18 marks)  \n(48 marks)\nAppendix 1 (Question 17) – Points forward schedule from prior \nyear’s audit file \nHobson sells and services domestic and commercial grass cutting machinery from its showrooms in a \nnumber of locations throughout Scotland and North England via its three divisions.  \nDomestic  \nThis division sells grass cutting machinery aimed at residential owners. This division has consistently \nperformed well and margins are generally high. However, in recent years, Hobson’s have found it \ndifficult to grow revenue from this side of the business. As a result, Hobsons have directed attention to \nchanging their product offering from traditional fuel mowers to new eco–friendly models. Industry \nanalysis supports that these products are premium–priced with margins around 5% higher than \ntraditional models and are attractive to clients, as they generally require less after sales servicing.  \nCommercial  \nCommercial sales are the largest revenue contributor in the business, however margins are generally \nlower than in the domestic business. The main clients include local councils and other private \nbusinesses responsible for the upkeep and maintenance of large–scale public and private landscapes \nand gardens.  \nParts and Servicing  \nThis division has been achieving significant growth in recent periods from commercial customers and \nthis is expected to continue. A large portion of Hobson’s Parts and Servicing inventory is small high–\nvalue parts. The company has a number of service vehicles and staff available to carry out repairs \neither at the workshops attached to each showroom or at clients’ sites.  \nDebt management  \nThe company offers payment terms of 30 days for domestic sales and 45 days for its other two \ndivisions. The company has a designated debt recovery team who regularly review outstanding debts \nand, where necessary, follow up overdue balances.  \nParts inventory counting  \nManagement decided that from February 20X8 a full inventory count of the parts inventory will not be \nundertaken at the year end. Instead, perpetual inventory counting will be undertaken on a monthly \nbasis which is planned to ensure that all items are counted at least once during the financial year.  \nNew showroom development and financing  \nIn February 20X8, the company acquired land and a building which has allowed them to expand into \nNorth England. The building will be re–developed into a new showroom and service department \nduring the first quarter of the 20X8–X9 financial year at a cost of £400,000. The expansion is being \nundertaken following the withdrawal from the area by a competitor which has created an opportunity \nfor Hobson.  \nThe company also plans to spend £50,000 on maintenance and refurbishment of three of its other \nexisting showrooms at the same time under its normal property refresh cycle. These costs will be \nfinanced by a bank loan repayable over 10 years which is secured on the building and is subject to \ncompliance with a covenant that the company must maintain a current ratio of at least 1.1:1 at all \ntimes.\nAppendix 2 (Question 17) – Results of audit data analytical routine: \nplanning analytics prepared by J Sinclair, data analysis manager \nA Objectives of audit data analytic routines – planning analytics  \ni. \nRevenue stream analysis for 10 month period to 30 November 20X8 (actual and budget), \n12 months ended 31 January 20X8 and extrapolated revenue for year ended 31 January \n20X9 for key revenue lines (Domestic, Commercial, Parts and Servicing)  \nii. \nCalculate gross profit margin per revenue stream (Domestic, Commercial, Parts and \nServicing) for actual 10 month period to 30 November 20X8, budget 10 month period to \n30 November 20X8 and actual year ended 31 January 20X8  \niii. \nSummarise net book value of property, plant and equipment between 30 November 20X8 \nand 31 January 20X8  \niv. \nCalculate current ratio at quarter end during 10 month period to 30 November 20X8 and \nas at 30 November 20X8  \nv. \nSummarise overdraft utilisation at quarter end during 10 month period to 30 November \n20X8 and as at 30 November 20X8  \nvi. \nCalculate performance ratios: Inventory and trade receivable days for period to 30 \nNovember 20X8, budget for period to 30 November 20X8 and actual for year ended 31 \nJanuary 20X8  \nB Cleansing of input data – completed J Sinclair 12 December 20X8. No issues to report.  \nC Completeness and integrity check of input data – completed by J Sinclair 12 December 20X8. \nNo issues to report. \nD D/E. Results and analysis \nOutput 1 – Revenue stream analysis for 10 month period to 30 November 20X8 (actual and budget), \n12 months ended 31 January 20X8 and extrapolated revenue for year ended 31 January 20X9 for key \nrevenue lines (Domestic, Commercial, Parts and Servicing)\nAppendix 2 (Question 17) (continued) \nOutput 2 – Gross profit margin per revenue stream (Domestic, Commercial, Parts and Servicing) for \nactual 10 month period to 30 November 20X8, budget 10 month period to 30 November 20X8 and \nactual year ended 31 January 20X8 \n \nOutput 3 – Comparison net book value (NBV) of property plant and equipment between 30 \nNovember 20X8 and 31 January 20X8\nAppendix 2 (Question 17) (continued) \nOutput 4 – Current ratio at quarter end during 10 month period to 30 November 20X8 and as at 30 \nNovember 20X8 \n \nOutput 5 – Overdraft utilisation at quarter end during 10 month period to 30 November 20X8 and as \nat 30 November 20X8\nOutput 6 – Performance ratios: Inventory and trade receivable days for period to 30 November 20X8, \nbudget for period to 30 November 20X8 and actual for year ended 31 January 20X8\nAppendix 3 (Question 19) – Notes of meeting with Jim Rae, finance \ndirector \nThe company has had a disappointing year at some showroom locations. In particular, the sales \ngrowth expected from the new showroom, which incurred significant capital investment and opened \nas planned at the end of March 20X8, has not yet been achieved. However, despite this disappointing \nstart for the new showroom, domestic sales, overall, have continued to perform well in 20X8.  \nThe main driver for growth in domestic sales has been a change in consumer habits. There is growing \ninterest in the market for eco–friendly lawnmowers, with consumers turning away from older ‘fossil \nfuelled’ models. The company has, therefore, increased its inventory of these new models to meet \nhigh demand and has incentivised sales staff to maximise sales in the final quarter to take advantage \nof the new market trend. Jim confirmed that the company has not made any provision against the \nvalue of older inventory as management are confident that the demand for the older models will \nremain sufficiently strong among buyers who continue to prefer the older technology.  \nSales of commercial machinery have been disappointing this year, largely due to external factors. \nCouncil budget cuts have had a direct impact on landscaping budgets and investment budgets, \nreducing equipment spend by some key customers. In some cases Hobson has approved significant \ndiscounts in order to achieve sales, especially at the new showroom where commercial demand has \nbeen significantly lower than expected. Following a request from a long–term customer, the company \nhas also sold a number of packaged combinations of domestic and commercial machinery to its \ncommercial customers. These packages are not available to domestic customers and are, therefore, \nonly recognised by the sales team under commercial revenue. This does not affect the recording of \nthe associated cost of sales.  \nRevenue from parts and servicing remains strong and there has been continued growth with the \ncompany maintaining consistent margin levels for this division. The fall in council spending on \ncommercial machinery has seen a number of councils increase spending on continued maintenance \nand servicing of older, existing equipment.  \nDue to a problem with specification of some commercial machinery, the fulfilment of a large customer \norder worth £200,000, which was expected to be fully fulfilled in November 20X8 has been delayed \nand is only 50% complete as at 30 November 20X8. It is expected that only 60% of the machinery will \nbe despatched by 31 January 20X9. The terms of the sale are full payment on completion of the \norder.  \nThe company had recently received correspondence from a local council planning department \ncomplaining that the development of the building in the North of England has not complied with the \nterms of the approved planning permission with unnecessary damage to an adjoining greenfield site. \nHobson disputes this and has contacted specialist planning consultants and lawyers for advice. \nHowever, there is a risk that, if the planning complaint is upheld, remedial landscaping work will be \nrequired, the cost of which has been estimated by the directors at £40,000.  \nThe company continues to utilise the overdraft facility in place with the company’s bank. The overdraft \nat 30 November 20X8 was £468,000 (31 January 20X8: £267,000). The limit on the overdraft is \n£500,000 and it is due for renewal in March 20X9. As planned the company received a loan from the \nbank to fund its capital redevelopment and refurbishment costs. The loan has been classified as a \nlong–term liability in the company’s management accounts and will be repayable in equal annual \ninstalments over the next 10 years commencing November 20X9.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 17 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 49</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-17-case-p049.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-17-case source PDF page 49\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 50</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-17-case-p050.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-17-case source PDF page 50\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 51</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-17-case-p051.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-17-case source PDF page 51\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 52</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-17-case-p052.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-17-case source PDF page 52\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 53</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-17-case-p053.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-17-case source PDF page 53\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 54</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-17-case-p054.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-17-case source PDF page 54\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 55</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-17-case-p055.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-17-case source PDF page 55\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [49, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55], "solution": [45, 46, 47, 48]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-4"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-018", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module9", "modules": ["Module5", "Module6", "Module9"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 18", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 30, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 56</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-18-q-p056.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-18-q source PDF page 56\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 49</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-18-a-p049.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-18-a source PDF page 49\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 50</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-18-a-p050.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-18-a source PDF page 50\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 51</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-18-a-p051.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-18-a source PDF page 51\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 18 (30 marks) \nIt is June 20X4. You are the audit senior on the audit of Pet–Trad Ltd (‘PTL’), a chain of five pet \nshops, located in retail parks around the UK. Inventories are supplied to the shops from a central \nwarehouse located beside the company’s head office in Manchester. The company is run by the \nManaging Director, Alf Wilson, and employs 70 people over its various locations. PTL is an \nestablished audit client of your firm and Alf is your main contact at the company. \nYou are planning the audit of inventories for the year ended 30 September 20X4. Alf has sent you \ndetails of the inventories held at each location (Appendix 1), including notes of the results of PTL’s \ninternal audit reviews in respect of inventory counting procedures. In your planning discussions with \nyour audit manager, Fred Pazmino, you agreed that, as part of the interim audit testing, your audit \nteam will attend a sample of the perpetual counts in order to assess the controls in place. Fred has \ntherefore asked you to recommend which of the counts your team will attend, together with an \nexplanation of why you plan to attend those. For reference, performance materiality has been set at \n£900,000. \nFollowing last year’s audit, Alf had a discussion with the audit partner, Lucy Simmons, where he \nexplained that PTL’s Audit Committee felt that the audit fee could be reduced if the audit team relied \nmore on the work of PTL’s internal audit department (‘IAD’). Fred has passed you an extract from the \naudit file on the structure of this department and the work that the department has carried out during \nthe financial year (Appendix 2). He has asked you to evaluate PTL’s internal audit department and \nconclude on whether reliance can be placed on the internal audit department for the purposes of the \nexternal audit work. \nRequired: \nPrepare information for Fred recommending, with reasons, which of the weekly inventory counts  the \naudit team should attend. \n(22 marks) \nPrepare information evaluating PTL’s internal audit department and concluding whether reliance can \nbe placed on the internal audit department for the purposes of the external audit \n(8 marks) \n(30 marks)\nAPPENDIX 1 (Question 18) – Information on inventory holdings and \ninternal audit visits obtained from Alf Wilson \nInternal audit randomly attends a sample of the counts that are carried out across the company, \nalthough not every store is visited each year. The warehouse counts are subject to at least one internal \naudit visit during each year because of the number of product lines and the value of the inventory in the \nwarehouse at any one point in time. \nLocation \nNumber of product \nlines \nApproximate \nValue of \ninventories \nIAD visited during \nthe period \nIAD findings \nduring visit \n \n \n£ \n \n \nWarehouse \n879 \n2,200,000 \nYes \nYes \nEdinburgh \n207 \n376,000 \nNo \nN/A \nInverness \n298 \n720,000 \nYes \nYes \nKensington \n156 \n872,000 \nNo \nN/A \nLiverpool \n648 \n1,300,000 \nYes \nNo \nManchester \n97 \n107,000 \nNo \nN/A \nNotes relating to IAD visits during the year \nSince September 20X3, internal audit performed reviews of the inventory counting procedures at the \ncentral warehouse, the Inverness store, and the Liverpool store, a new superstore that has opened \nsince September 20X3. Reviews at the warehouse and at the Inverness store were carried out by Julie \nKeane (internal audit manager), and the review at Liverpool was carried out by Alice Jenkins (internal audit \ntrainee). \nAs in previous years, internal audit findings are rated on a tiered basis according to PTL’s view of the level \nof risk associated with the finding. The tiers used are serious, moderate and low risk and these ratings have \nnot changed from the previous year. \nDetails of internal audit findings \nAt the warehouse, two moderate risk findings were raised: \n• \nThe fact that movements in inventories were not stopped during the count, although there \nwas no evidence that any misstatement of inventories resulted from this; and \n• \nThe use of staff that had recently joined the company to carry out the inventory counts. These \nindividuals lacked experience. \nIn Inverness, there were two moderate risk findings: \n• \nA lack of documented inventory count instructions; and \n• \nA lack of documentation of the results of the inventory count.  \nNo findings were detected at the Liverpool superstore.\nAPPENDIX 2 (Question 18) – Extract from Audit File \nThe internal audit department (‘IAD’) is a relatively small department of four individuals under the \ndepartment director, Catherine Smilie. Three have the job title of manager and are qualified \naccountants (Steve Harris, John Anderson and Julie Keane) from a variety of institutes (ICAS, ACCA \nand CIPFA) and the fourth, Alice Jenkins, is a trainee in her first job since leaving university. Alice is \ncurrently undergoing chartered accountancy training. She was hired because part of her degree \ncourse was in computer science and none of the other members of the department have any formal \nIT qualifications although all have some practical experience of computerised systems. \nCatherine has worked in the IAD at the company for nine years, having been hired by the original \nFinance Director following the detection of a minor fraud. Steve has been with the company for seven \nyears, following eight years spent working in a food retail environment. John and Julie have both been \nworking with the company for three years, having been hired from one of the company’s direct \ncompetitors. \nAll areas of the company are audited by the IAD following a risk–based plan. High risk areas are \nreviewed every year, medium risk every second year and low risk areas are examined every three \nyears. Work is carried out by the audit managers using the trainee for assistance where needed. The \ntrainee’s work is reviewed and signed off by the specific manager covering the audit area. Managers’ \nwork papers are reviewed by another manager. The managers write their reports at the conclusion of \naudit fieldwork, agree their conclusions with the relevant staff members being audited, and then have \ntheir work reviewed by Catherine. PTL then implements the recommendations in the reports. On a \nquarterly basis, Catherine reports formally to the Audit Committee, who are also responsible for \nsetting the remuneration of all of the IAD staff and setting the IAD annual plan.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 18 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 56</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-18-case-p056.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-18-case source PDF page 56\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 57</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-18-case-p057.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-18-case source PDF page 57\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 58</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-18-case-p058.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-18-case source PDF page 58\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [56, 57, 58], "solution": [49, 50, 51]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-5", "aa-module-6", "aa-module-9"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-019", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module6", "modules": ["Module4", "Module6"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 19", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 50, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 59</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-19-q-p059.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-19-q source PDF page 59\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 52</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-19-a-p052.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-19-a source PDF page 52\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 53</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-19-a-p053.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-19-a source PDF page 53\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 54</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-19-a-p054.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-19-a source PDF page 54\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 19 (50 marks) \nYou are the audit manager for Magma Laser Components Ltd (‘Magma’), a family–run company \noperating in the North of England. Your firm, Stevens, Selby & Co, has audited the company for a \nnumber of years and you are planning the audit of inventory and other issues for the year ending 28 \nFebruary 20X7. \nMagma was founded ten years ago and specialises in designing, developing and manufacturing a \ncomprehensive range of laser products for scientific, industrial, medical and defence applications. \nOver the years, the company has continued to grow and widen its product range and now operates \nfrom a number of sites across England. The three main manufacturing sites each specialise in the \nproduction of specific types of lasers. As well as a standard product line. Magma is able to customise \nproducts for specific client needs. These manufacturing sites are supported by a central warehouse \nwhich holds all raw material components, and a development centre which specialises in product \ndevelopment and testing. \nCraig Stevens, the audit partner, recently had a meeting with John Lloyd, Magma’s finance director, to \nassist with the audit planning. He has provided you with an extract of the latest management accounts \nand related commentary (Appendix 1). Craig has asked you to prepare paragraphs suitable for \ninclusion in the audit strategy memorandum in which you need to evaluate the audit risks relating to \nMagma’s inventory. \nDuring the year, Magma revised its purchasing process to include the ability to recognise a liability for \nraw materials on arrival at its warehouse. This is now recognised through a Goods Received Not \nInvoiced accrual (‘GRNI’) in the nominal ledger. As this is a new process, Craig has asked you to \nreview the GRNI process notes, provided by John (Appendix 2), and to draft paragraphs suitable for \ninclusion in a management letter advising on any weaknesses, analysing the implications to Magma, \nand recommending improvements in the processing of the GRNI. \nRequired: \n1. Evaluate the audit risks relating to Magma’s inventory. \n(20 marks) \n2. Prepare information identifying the weaknesses, analysing the implications and recommending \nimprovements in the processing of GRNI. \n(30 marks) \n(50 marks)\nAPPENDIX 1 (QUESTION 19) – Management accounts extract and \ncommentary \nInventory \nNote \nActual 30 November \n20X6 \nBudget 28 February \n20X7 \nPrior Year 28 \nFebruary 20X6 \n \n \n£ \n£ \n£ \nRaw material \n1 \n2,105,670 \n1,680,000 \n1,560,000 \nWork in progress \n2 \n354,600 \n55,000 \n60,000 \nFinished goods \n3 \n1,344,830 \n1,085,000 \n1,010,000 \nTotal inventory \n \n3,805,100 \n2,820,000 \n2,630,000 \n \n \n \n \n \nKey Inventory KPIs \n \n \n \n \nInventory turnover \n \n5.9 \n7.1 \n7.5 \n \n \n \n \n \nInventory days \n \n61 days \n51 days \n48 days \nOverall position \nIncrease in inventory reflects the increase in product ranges manufactured, new products launched, \nand growth of business through 20X6. \nThe forecasted value of inventory at 28 February 20X7 is £3.55m. \nNote: \n1. Raw materials \nThe company has received recent correspondence from a supplier recalling a number of components \nthat have been found to be faulty. The supplier has indicated that these goods should be returned, \nand a credit note will be issued. An initial review has identified that the majority of these items, valued at \napproximately £220,000, is still held as raw material inventory. Some items were used in production and \nare included in finished goods inventory with a total value of £250,000. The cost of remedial work to \nensure that these products are safe has been estimated at £30,000. \nRaw materials include an estimate of £254,000 for a bulk order of silica powder received but not yet \ninvoiced. \n2. Work in progress (‘WIP’) \nWIP is estimated on a percentage completion basis. WIP is located primarily at the manufacturing site \nin Leeds, due to a longer production process as more complex product ranges are manufactured at \nthis site. At other sites, WIP balances are minimised at each quarter end, to coincide with inventory \ncounting, through effective production planning. \nWIP balances at quarter ends have historically been around £50,000 to £60,000 and the increase at \n30 November 20X6 resulted from an issue at the manufacturing site in Leeds where a problem \ncausing delays in production meant that certain products were only 60% completed at the time of the \ninventory count. In addition, this problem resulted in a number of products being scrapped. This was a \n‘one–off’ event. \n3. Finished goods \nThe company has continued to widen its product lines during the year, introducing several new \nproducts and also enhancing established products. It is estimated that production has increased by \n20% in the past year and that maximum production capacity has been reached at some of the \nmanufacturing sites. Sales of the new products have not been as high as budgeted resulting in a \nbuild–up of inventory at 30 November 20X6. Management is confident, however, that the sales \npotential is strong for these new lines.\nAPPENDIX 1 (QUESTION 19) (continued) \n4. Inventory provision \nMagma makes a 100% provision against items that are considered obsolete. This is determined by a \ndetailed review of inventory items with no movements over a 12–month period or where products are \nknown to have been replaced by new product lines. \n5. Other matters affecting inventory \nA new competitor has entered the market offering a directly comparable product to one of Magma’s \nkey lines, ‘Red Optical Lasers’ (‘ROL’). The competitor’s product comes at a slightly higher \nspecification, but lower price than the ROL product. Management has identified that sales of ROL fell \n10% in the six months to 30 November 20X6 and the directors continue to monitor developments from \nthis competitor. This product normally makes up 15% of the value of finished goods inventory at any \ntime. \nIn November 20X6, one of Magma’s customers, GTZ Technologies (‘GTZ’), entered administration. \nThere are no amounts outstanding in accounts receivable from GTZ. However, Magma has \napproximately £50,000 of products in finished goods inventory ready for dispatch in January 20X7 to \nmeet an outstanding order. This product had been customised specifically to meet GTZ requirements \nand Magma does not expect that this can now be sold.\nAPPENDIX 2 (QUESTION 19) – Goods received not invoiced accrual \n(‘GRNI’) process notes \nGoods received in the warehouse are booked into inventory using a hand–held scanner by members \nof the warehouse team. At the same time, a posting is automatically made to the GRNI account in the \nnominal ledger using standard prices stored in the nominal ledger together with the quantities \nreceived per goods received note. The standard prices are reviewed on an annual basis. \nA number of laser components are received in bulk in specialist containers which cannot be opened \nfor safety reasons. For these products a member of the warehouse team estimates the overall \nquantity based on weight and manually inputs into inventory and the GRNI. Posting for both the \nautomated and manual input of the GRNI is as follows: \nDr Inventory  \nCr GRNI \nWhen a purchase invoice is received by the accounts department, it is matched to the GRNI and the \npurchase invoice amount is posted to trade payables in the purchase ledger. Any differences between \nthe GRNI and the invoice are posted to an inventory suspense account in the nominal ledger. At the \nend of the financial year the balance on the suspense account will be cleared and transferred to the \nprofit and loss account. The posting on matching is as follows: \nDr GRNI \nDr / Cr inventory suspense account (for any difference)  \nCr Purchase ledger / Trade payables \nWhere goods are purchased in foreign currency, a standard exchange rate is used for recording \ngoods into the GRNI and is set at the start of the financial year. When the purchase invoice is \nreceived this is translated at the same standard exchange rate to ensure consistency with the GRNI \naccrual. \nThe purchase ledger clerk carries out a reconciliation of the purchase ledger to the GRNI each month \nto ensure that all items in the GRNI for which invoices have been received have been cleared and to \ncheck for any items for which the invoice is still outstanding. Once the reconciliation is complete, the \npurchase ledger clerk files the reconciliation away in date order.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 19 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 59</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-19-case-p059.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-19-case source PDF page 59\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 60</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-19-case-p060.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-19-case source PDF page 60\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 61</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-19-case-p061.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-19-case source PDF page 61\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 62</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-19-case-p062.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-19-case source PDF page 62\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [59, 60, 61, 62], "solution": [52, 53, 54]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-4", "aa-module-6"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-020", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module10", "modules": ["Module10"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 20", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 50, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 63</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-20-q-p063.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-20-q source PDF page 63\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 55</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-20-a-p055.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-20-a source PDF page 55\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 56</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-20-a-p056.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-20-a source PDF page 56\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 57</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-20-a-p057.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-20-a source PDF page 57\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 58</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-20-a-p058.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-20-a source PDF page 58\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 20 (50 marks) \nIt is August 20X3 and you are an audit senior working on the audit of Crown Insurance Ltd (‘Crown’), \nan online insurance company. Crown’s year end was 30 June 20X3 and the audit report is due to be \nsigned by 30 September 20X3. The board of directors (‘the board’) has requested an accelerated \ntimescale for the audit this year in order to focus on other priorities. The audit fieldwork is complete \nand you are reviewing Crown’s cashflow forecasts and the related management commentary \nprovided by the finance director (‘FD’) to support the assumption that the company will continue as a \ngoing concern. \nCrown has been established for 15 years and the business is run from its head office in Swansea. It \noffers a range of insurance products with customers purchasing directly through its website. The \ncurrent products it offers are car and home insurance. The amounts that customers pay annually for \neach policy are known as insurance premiums. \nYour audit partner has asked you to analyse Crown’s cashflow forecasts and evaluate the \nreasonableness of the assumptions that Crown’s management team have used when preparing these \nforecasts. As part of your review, you have also been asked to advise on further specific audit \nevidence to be obtained to ensure appropriate assurance over management’s going concern \nassumption. \nThe FD has provided you with Crown’s quarterly cashflow projections covering the period 1 July 20X3 \nuntil 30 June 20X4 as well as full year actual cashflows for the year ended 30 June 20X3 which your \naudit partner has asked you to use for your evaluation (Appendix 1). Your audit partner has already \nhighlighted to Crown that it should prepare monthly cashflow forecasts and extend the forecast period \nto 30 September 20X4 so these issues do not need to be mentioned in your analysis. \nRequired: \n1. Analyse the cashflow forecasts provided and evaluate the reasonableness of the assumptions \nand information given. \n(26 marks) \n2. Advise on further specific audit evidence to be obtained to ensure appropriate assurance over the \nissues identified in requirement 1 and management’s going concern assumption. \n(24 marks) \n(50 marks)\nAPPENDIX 1 (Question 20) \nThe FD has provided the following cashflow forecast extract which has been used as the basis for \nmanagement’s assumption that the company will continue as a going concern: \n \nActual Year \nend \nForecast Q1 \nending \nForecast Q2 \nending \nForecast Q3 \nending \nForecast Q4 \nending \nForec\nYear end\n \n30 Jun 20X3 \n£m \n30 Sep 20X3 \n£m \n31 Dec 20X3 \n£m \n31 Mar 20X4 \n£m \n30 Jun 20X4 \n£m \n30 Jun 20\n£\nCash inflows \n \n \n \n \n \nCar premiums \n106.4 \n30.5 \n32.7 \n31.9 \n29.5 \n124\nHouse premiums \n36.4 \n9.5 \n10.6 \n9.7 \n8.4 \n38\nPet premiums \n– \n0.7 \n1.1 \n1.6 \n2.4 \n5\nBank loan \n– \n– \n10.0 \n– \n– \n10\nTotal inflows \n142.8 \n40.7 \n54.4 \n43.2 \n40.3 \n178\nCash outflows \n \n \n \n \n \nClaims paid \n26.4 \n6.3 \n3.4 \n7.9 \n4.3 \n2\nRent and office costs \n7.5 \n1.9 \n1.9 \n1.9 \n2.1 \n7\nPayroll costs \n51.5 \n13.3 \n13.3 \n13.3 \n13.3 \n53\nInsurance premiums \n23.3 \n5.3 \n5.8 \n5.6 \n5.3 \n22\nInterest expenses \n– \n– \n– \n0.1 \n0.1 \n0\nTotal outflows \n108.7 \n26.8 \n24.4 \n28.8 \n25.1 \n105\n \n \n \n \n \n \nTotal net cash inflows \n34.1 \n13.9 \n30.0 \n14.4 \n15.2 \n73\nNotes to the cashflow forecast: \nCar premiums \nTo prepare this forecast Crown’s management team have used the average growth they have seen in \ncar premiums over the past ten years. The FD noted that the UK has seen steady growth in the \nnumber of cars on the roads in the past 25 years however it has recently been published that the \nincrease year on year is slowing due to many households reducing down to one car as a result of an \nincrease in working from home and people trying to be more environmentally friendly. \nHouse premiums \nManagement has set a prudent increase of 5% on household premiums based on historic \nperformance. It was noted by the management team during 20X2 that a number of customers were \nswitching their home insurance away from Crown and taking advantage of better deals that a number \nof high street banks were offering when people took out mortgages. \nPet insurance project \nCrown is in the process of launching pet insurance due to the increasing number of pets people have \nacquired as a result of the increase in working from home. This is a new product and the development \nproject is still on–going. The launch was planned for July 20X3, however there have been some \ndelays in decision making and the forecasted launch of the product is now February 20X4. The \ncashflow figures and growth assumptions have been verified by an independent expert who believes \nthey are prudent and fair.\nAPPENDIX 1 (Question 20) (continued) \nLoan \nThe board is in final discussions with the bank regarding a loan of £10m to support the pet insurance \nproject. It has highlighted to the FD that it expects the interest rate to be 3% fixed over three years \nwhich was similar to the previous loan that Crown had four years ago (now fully repaid). They expect \nthe paperwork to be completed imminently and the loan to be drawn down in October 20X3, with \ninterest payments starting in the following month. \nClaims paid \nWhen customers make a claim, it must go through a review process prior to payment. The \nmanagement team used a summary report from the claims team to prepare the cashflow forecast and \nused the “claims confirmed” figure. The “claims unconfirmed” are any claims that need extra \ninvestigation and historically 40% of these are paid to customers. \n \nPayment \nSep 20X3 \n£m \nPayment \nDec 20X3 \n£m \nPayment \nMar 20X3 \n£m \nPayment \nJun 20X4 \n£m \nPayment \nSep 20X4 \n£m \nPayment \nDec 20X4 \n£m \nClaims – confirmed \n6.3 \n3.4 \n7.9 \n4.3 \n8.2 \n2.2 \nClaims – unconfirmed \n1.6 \n0.9 \n2.3 \n1.1 \n2.5 \n0.5 \nRent and office costs \nRent and office costs are split 75% rent, 25% office costs (utilities) and the FD has provided you with \nthe following breakdown: \n \nActual Y/E 30 June 20X3 \n£m \nForecast Y/E 30 June 20X4 \n£m \nRent \n5.60 \n5.75 \nOffice \n1.85 \n2.04 \nTotal \n7.45 \n7.79 \nDue to an increase in energy costs during 20X2, it was agreed to increase its utilities costs in the \nforecast by 15% on 20X3 figures. Crown has also received a rent increase notice which is effective \nfrom October 20X3 and will increase its rent by £50,000 a month. \nInsurance premium tax \nInsurance premium tax (‘IPT’) is a tax collected from customers and paid to the Government by insurance \ncompanies. Customers pay IPT as part of their premium and Crown pays what is due to the Government \neach quarter based on the premiums it has received. There are two rates of IPT, a standard rate of 13% \nand a higher rate of 20%, dependent on the type of policy. For the cashflow forecast the management \nteam has assumed a 13% rate based on the total premium per quarter.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 20 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 63</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-20-case-p063.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-20-case source PDF page 63\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 64</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-20-case-p064.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-20-case source PDF page 64\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 65</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-20-case-p065.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-20-case source PDF page 65\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [63, 64, 65], "solution": [55, 56, 57, 58]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-10"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-021", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module7", "modules": ["Module7"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 21", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 39, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 66</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-21-q-p066.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-21-q source PDF page 66\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 59</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-21-a-p059.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-21-a source PDF page 59\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 60</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-21-a-p060.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-21-a source PDF page 60\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 61</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-21-a-p061.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-21-a source PDF page 61\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 21 (39 marks) \nYou are an audit senior at Larch and Fir LLP (‘LF’) working on the year end audit of the financial \nstatements of Hilltop Wood Limited (‘Hilltop’) for the year ended 30 June 20X7. \nHilltop’ s principal activity is the processing of timber in Scotland and the sale of timber in the UK and \noverseas. Hilltop operates one of the largest sawmills in the UK from a port on the west coast of \nScotland. The wood is transported by sea to the sawmill where it is processed from the felled tree into \nkiln dried timber, machined white wood timber for builders, pallets, packaging, decking and fencing. \nThe by–products from the process are sold for biomass fuel. \nDue to the nature of the work, the company has substantial investments in property, plant and \nequipment (‘PPE’). You are currently working on the PPE section of the audit file, specifically the audit \nof depreciation. \nDuring the year, Hilltop purchased a 64–metre motorised vessel, a tug and a barge to assist in the \ntransportation of the felled timber to the port local to the sawmill. This is a new and exciting \ndevelopment for the company as previously all timber was transported by a third–party company on \nbehalf of Hilltop. \nThe audit planning concluded that the controls over depreciation are robust. As such, the planned \naudit approach is a substantive analytical review of depreciation, with testing at an individual asset \nlevel. \nTo facilitate the review, your audit manager, Sally Birch, has provided you with an extract of Hilltop’s \nasset register and the relevant accounting policies (Appendix 1). Following a meeting with the Hilltop \nfinancial accountant, Sally has emailed you a summary of her meeting notes (Appendix 2). Sally has \nalso informed you that the FX rates used by the client have already been considered, agreed to an \nindependent external source and have been determined to be appropriate. Therefore, she has asked \nyou not to consider FX as a potential reason for any variances. \nYou note from the audit file that the tolerable error has been set at £6,000. \nRequired: \nUsing substantive analytical procedures, analyse the depreciation charge for the year for each \nindividual asset, advising, with reasons, whether the charge is reasonable or if further audit work is \nrequired. \n(19 marks) \nAnalyse the potential reasons for any variances between the expected and actual depreciation \ncharge. \n(12 marks) \nRecommend what further procedures would need to be performed to corroborate the reasons for any \nidentified variances. \n(8 marks) \n(39 marks)\nAPPENDIX 1 (Question 21) – Extract from Hilltop’s asset register at \n30 June 20X7 and relevant accounting policies \nExtract of accounting policies for Property, Plant & Equipment Property \nPlant & Equipment is measured at historical cost less accumulated depreciation. \nHistorical cost includes expenditure that is directly attributable to bringing the asset to the location and \ncondition for it to be capable of operating in the manner intended by the management. \nIn line with accounting standards, assets purchased in a foreign currency are translated at the \nexchange rate on the date of purchase and are not revalued at each year end. \nThe company adds to the carrying amount of an item of Property Plant & Equipment the cost of \nreplacing part of any item if the replacement is expected to provide incremental future benefits to the \ncompany and the part which has been replaced is disposed of from the carrying amount. \nDepreciation is charged in the first full month of ownership; no depreciation is charged if the transfer \nof the asset did not occur on the first day of the month. \nDepreciation is charged on the following basis: \nLand \n \n \nNot depreciated \nBuildings \n \nStraight line over 20 years \nBoats and Equipment \n10% on a diminishing balance \nPlant and Machinery \nStraight line over 8 years \nMotor Vehicles  \nStraight line over 5 years \nExtract from Hilltop’s asset register at 30 June 20X7 \nAsset \nDate \npurchased / \nrevalued \nCost at 30 \nJune 20X7 \nUseful Life \n(years) \nDepreciation \ncharge for the \nyear to 30 \nJune 20X7 \nLand and Buildings \n \n \n \n \nSawmill land and various buildings \n30/6/20X4 \n£1,650,000 \n20 \n£82,500 \nBoats and Equipment \n \n \n \n \nTug \n01/01/20X7 \n£700,892 \nn/a \n£35,050 \nBarge \n03/02/20X7 \n£2,000,000 \nn/a \n£83,335 \nPlant and Machinery \n \n \n \n \nBark stripping machine \n01/04/20X1 \n£1,450,000 \n8 \n£185,000 \nIndustrial saw \n01/09/20X5 \n£1,000,000 \n8 \n£125,000 \nElectric car \n01/07/20X6 \n£55,000 \n5 \n£11,000\nAPPENDIX 2 (Question 21) – Meeting notes \nClient: Hilltop Wood Limited Date: 20 July 20X7 \nPresent: Hilltop financial accountant \nPrepared by: Sally Birch \n1. The tug was purchased second hand in Greece for €785,000 and was refitted in a Greek shipyard \nto make it fit for purpose for Hilltop, at a further cost of €300,000. Hilltop paid for the vessel and \nrefit on receipt of the Vessel Safety Certificate on 1 January 20X7. The costs have been \ntranslated at the exchange rate on 1 January 20X7 of £1 to €1.12. \n2. The barge to be towed by the tug was purchased from a Swedish manufacturer for a value of \nSwedish Krona (SEK) 25,300,000 following the receipt of the Vessel Safety Certificate on 3 \nFebruary 20X7. The cost has been translated at the exchange rate on 3 February 20X7 of £1 to \nSEK12.65. \n3. Much of the equipment used by Hilltop is modular and so can be bolted together to form a \nproduction line of the processes required. This allows modules to be upgraded as and when the \ntechnology has improved. A major module of the bark stripping machine was removed and \nreplaced during the year and the correct adjustments have been included in the cost of the asset. \n4. A purchase order was raised for a new fleet car on 1 July 20X6. However, due to manufacturing \ndelays on the continent it took a further eight months for the delivery of the car from the \nmanufacturer. The keys were handed over on 1 March 20X7. \n5. The sawmill land and buildings were purchased 15 years ago after the decline of the ship building \nindustry in the town. It was revalued at 30 June 20X4 with the surveyor valuing the land at \n£950,000.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 21 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 66</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-21-case-p066.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-21-case source PDF page 66\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 67</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-21-case-p067.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-21-case source PDF page 67\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 68</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-21-case-p068.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-21-case source PDF page 68\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [66, 67, 68], "solution": [59, 60, 61]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-7"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-022", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module1", "modules": ["Module1"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 22", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 25, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 69</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-22-q-p069.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-22-q source PDF page 69\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 62</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-22-a-p062.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-22-a source PDF page 62\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 63</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-22-a-p063.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-22-a source PDF page 63\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 22 (25 marks) \nYou are an audit senior at Pullar and Rutledge LLP (‘P&H’), a firm of chartered accountants and \nregistered auditors. You are about to begin work on the audit of Fiver Staffing plc (’Fiver’) for the year \nending 31 December 20X9. The company is an international recruitment business headquartered in \nEdinburgh, UK that specialises in recruitment for the life–science sector. Fiver was incorporated 30 \nyears ago and has seen large levels of growth in recent years, listing on the London Stock Exchange \n(LSE) in 20X4. \nFiver has been an audit client for 8 years; however, this is your first year working on this audit and you \nare feeling quite nervous as you know that the timeline for the year–end audit fieldwork is quite short. \nYou have been reviewing previous years’ audit files to bring yourself up to speed on Fiver, and you \nnoticed that there is a strong level of continuity on the audit as the audit manager, Liam Welsh, has \nworked on the audit for three years and the engagement partner, Cathy Quinn, has been the \nengagement partner on this audit since Fiver became an audit client. This is reassuring to you, as you \nfeel you will be well supported on this audit and benefit from their expertise. \nYou know that Fiver is an important client for P&H, and that the firm provides various services to Fiver \nin addition to statutory audit. You find a breakdown of last year’s audit fee and other fee income \n(Appendix) as you are studying the prior year audit file – as you examine this you remember a \nconversation you heard last Wednesday between Cathy and Tim Kennedy (P&H Tax Partner) where \nthey were discussing the potential provision of tax services for Fiver, including the preparation of \ncurrent or deferred tax calculations. \nA week before the year–end audit fieldwork commences, you have a meeting with Liam to discuss \nyour role and expectations for the audit. Your responsibilities will cover a wide range of challenging \nareas, including review of investment impairment and management’s going concern forecast. At the \nend of the meeting, you raise a concern with Liam – you know that your cousin holds some shares in \nFiver, and you are wondering if this constitutes an independence issue. Liam tells not to worry – he \nhimself has a shareholding in Fiver, and this has never been an issue on the audit. \nOn your first day on site at the Fiver offices you and Liam have a meeting with Fiver’s financial \ncontroller, John Quinn, who introduces himself as Cathy’s husband. After discussing various matters \nJohn gives you a Fiver branded pen and notebook. You are pleased with this and even more \ndelighted when John mentions that, at the end of the audit, he might be able to get you tickets for an \nupcoming international rugby match. \nRequired: \nEvaluate the factors which may threaten the independence of Fiver’s audit of the financial statements \nfor the year ending 31 December 20X9. Briefly explain how each threat could be managed. \n(25 marks)\nAppendix (Question 22) – Fee income from Fiver", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 22 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 69</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-22-case-p069.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-22-case source PDF page 69\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 70</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-22-case-p070.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-22-case source PDF page 70\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [69, 70], "solution": [62, 63]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-1"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-023", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module10", "modules": ["Module10"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 23", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 27, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 71</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-23-case-p071.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-23-case source PDF page 71\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 64</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-23-a-p064.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-23-a source PDF page 64\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 65</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-23-a-p065.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-23-a source PDF page 65\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 23 (27 marks) \nYou are an audit manager of Edmonds and Sinclair LLP, a firm of registered auditors. You are \nworking on the audit of Golden Advisory Ltd (‘Golden’) a management consultancy company, who \nbecame a client of the audit firm last year. \nAs it is your first year as audit manager on Golden, the audit partner Caroline Miller has directed you \nto the prior year audit file to help bring you up to speed. When reading the prior year audit file you \nnotice the following: \ni. \nThe acceptance risk analysis included an evaluation of independence risks faced by the \nengagement team. However, where threats were identified, no safeguards were documented. \nii. \nThe engagement team only had an engagement partner, engagement manager and a junior staff \nmember on the team. The planning documentation indicated that the engagement required an \nengagement partner, manager, senior and two junior staff but due to the firm’s resource \navailability this was not the case. \niii. \nRevenue was considered to be a high–risk area in the prior year audit, but work on revenue does \nnot appear to have been reviewed. \niv. \nA fair value assessment of Golden’s properties was carried out by an external expert. Concerns \nhad been raised on the audit file over whether the approach taken by the expert was in \naccordance with relevant financial reporting standards, but the engagement team failed to discuss \nthis with the audit firm’s in–house technical team. \nRequired \nExplain any quality management issues from the prior year audit of Golden and the potential impact \nthese may have. Recommend the actions to be taken by the audit firm to rectify these quality \nmanagement issues. \n(27 marks)", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 23 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 71</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-23-case-p071.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-23-case source PDF page 71\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [71], "solution": [64, 65]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-10"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-024", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module10", "modules": ["Module10"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 24", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 25, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 72</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-24-case-p072.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-24-case source PDF page 72\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 73</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-24-case-p073.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-24-case source PDF page 73\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 66</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-24-a-p066.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-24-a source PDF page 66\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 67</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-24-a-p067.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-24-a source PDF page 67\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 68</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-24-a-p068.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-24-a source PDF page 68\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 24 (25 marks) \nIt is May 20X4 and you are an audit senior working to conclude the audit of Renew Clothing plc \n(‘Renew’), a global chain of sustainable clothing stores, for the year ended 31 March 20X4. \nYou are in the final days of the audit and are reviewing the minutes of a meeting (Appendix) between \nwith your audit partner (John Wilkie) your audit manager (Jess Jones) and the independent \nengagement quality reviewer (EQR) partner (Cara Henderson) where various matters were \ndiscussed. \nFrom these matters Jess has asked you to analyse which should be included in a “points forward \nschedule”, and which should be communicated to those charged with governance. \nRequired \n1. Explain the purpose of a points forward schedule and analyse, with reasons, which specific points \nfrom the meeting should be included in the points forward schedule. \n(12 marks) \n2. Analyse, with reasons, which specific points from the meeting require to be reported to those \ncharged with governance. \n(13 marks) \n(25 marks)\nAppendix (Question 24) Minutes of meeting \n1. John confirmed he intended to sign the audit report on 30 May in line with the previously agreed \ntimetable. He commended Jess and the team’s efforts which have enabled them to be in a \nposition to sign, despite significant delays in obtaining information from Renew at the start of the \naudit. \n2. Jess informed John that the team discussed the new product line of sustainably made shoes with \nLaura McIntyre (financial director of Renew). Through enquiry and other audit procedures they \nhave gained evidence that Renew are on track to begin production in October 20X4, following \ndevelopment this year. \n3. John confirmed that he has completed review of the revenue audit workpapers. He commented \nthat, while the quality of work was good, it was time–consuming to review due to the high number \nof samples which had been performed manually. \n4. Cara informed John and Jess that she had performed reviews of the financial statements, \nsignificant judgements and consultations which had taken place. This led to a discussion on the \ninventory provision in the financial statements. The estimates used by the directors were highly \njudgemental and material. \n5. Cara also raised for discussion the matter of a theft identified by the audit team following a \nphysical verification of new equipment purchased during the year. Jess confirmed that this was \nfound to be an isolated incident, but that Renew were taking legal action against the employee \nsuspected of stealing the equipment. \n6. Jess shared an update she had received from Laura along with a copy of Renew’s recent \nEnvironment Agency report after an unauthorised discharge of contaminated waste into a \nmarshland near one of Renew’s factories. Renew has included an appropriate provision for \nestimated legal costs in the financial statements. \n7. John reminded Jess that a weakness had been identified during internal controls testing, and \nasked her to ensure that the following weakness was appropriately documented on the audit file: \nWhen testing the sales system, the sales clerk informed the audit team that sales could be made \nby individuals outside the sales team. In such situations, sales orders are not always completed.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 24 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 72</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-24-case-p072.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-24-case source PDF page 72\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 73</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-24-case-p073.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-24-case source PDF page 73\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [72, 73], "solution": [66, 67, 68]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-10"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-025", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module8", "modules": ["Module8"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 25", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 17, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 74</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-25-q-p074.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-25-q source PDF page 74\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 69</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-25-a-p069.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-25-a source PDF page 69\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 70</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-25-a-p070.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-25-a source PDF page 70\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 25 (17 marks) \nYou are an audit manager planning the audit of PBN Services Ltd (‘PBN’) for the year ended 31 July \n20X7. You have agreed that a substantive approach is appropriate. PBN is an established audit client \nwhich supplies and distributes plumbing and heating products. You have received the following \nextract from PBN's management accounts as at 31 July 20X7 showing property, plant and equipment \n(‘PPE’):  \nProperty    \nComputer \nsystems    \nVehicles    \nTotal  \nCost \n  \n  \n  \n \nAt 1 August 20X6 \n10,256    \n1,060   \n565   \n11,881 \nAdditions \n    \n    \n182    \n182  \nDisposals \n(320)    \n    \n–    \n(320)  \nAt 31 July 20X7 \n9,936    \n1,060    \n747    \n11,743  \nDepreciation \n  \n  \n  \n \nAt 1 August 20X6 \n2,650   \n424   \n226   \n3,300 \nCharge for the year \n530    \n190    \n105    \n825  \nEliminated on disposal \n(320)    \n    \n    \n(320)  \nAt 31 July 20X7 \n2,860    \n614    \n331    \n3,805  \nCarrying amount \n    \n    \n    \n  \nAt 1 August 20X6 \n7,606    \n636    \n339    \n8,581  \n    \n    \n    \n  \nAt 31 July 20X7 \n7,076    \n446    \n416    \n7,938  \nAnother member of the engagement team is considering tests for leased assets, revaluations and \nimpairment, and so you are not required to consider these areas. \nRequired:  \nDesign tests of detail that should be performed to gather evidence to support the PPE balances from \nthe information provided above.  \n(17 marks)", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 25 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 74</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-25-case-p074.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-25-case source PDF page 74\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [74], "solution": [69, 70]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-8"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-026", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module12", "modules": ["Module12"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 26", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 30, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 75</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-26-case-p075.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-26-case source PDF page 75\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 76</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-26-case-p076.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-26-case source PDF page 76\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 71</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-26-a-p071.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-26-a source PDF page 71\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 72</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-26-a-p072.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-26-a source PDF page 72\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 73</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-26-a-p073.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-26-a source PDF page 73\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 26 (30 marks) \nYoga Fanatics Limited \nYou are an audit senior with Black & Miller, a firm of registered auditors. You have recently been \nseconded to the forensic audit department, a relatively new and growing branch of Black and Miller, to \nwork on an upcoming forensic audit of Yoga Fanatics Limited (Yoga). \nIt has become clear to senior management at Black & Miller that many employees throughout the \nbusiness do not understand how a forensic audit differs from other types of audit. Therefore, the first \npiece of work which you are required to do is to prepare a briefing to all staff evaluating the distinct \nconsiderations and requirements which need to be considered when undertaking a forensic audit. \nJames, the forensic audit manager, has provided you with a brief containing details regarding a \nsuspected fraud at Yoga (Appendix). As the forensic audit department is relatively new, there is not \nyet software in place to generate a tailored programme of audit procedures. James has therefore \nasked you to generate a programme of audit procedures tailored to the situation at Yoga. \nQUESTIONS \nQuestion 1 \nEvaluate the distinct considerations and requirements when undertaking a forensic audit. \n(10 marks) \nQuestion 2 \nUsing Appendix 1, design an audit programme to meet the required brief of the forensic audit \nengagement. \n(20 marks) \n(30 marks)\nAppendix (Question 26) \nBackground information \nYoga is a chain of yoga studios founded in Glasgow which now operates across the UK. The \ncompany’s revenue is primarily generated from the sale of memberships which allow members to \nattend yoga classes. Memberships are monthly rolling or annual, starting from the day the member \nsigns up. The company has a year end of 31 December 20X6. \nThe CEO of Yoga is extremely passionate about yoga and spent the final three weeks of 20X6 on a \nyoga retreat in Bali. The CFO has been unable to attend to business matters in recent months due to \nill health and Carrie the financial controller, has been acting as interim CFO in their absence. With this \nincreased responsibility, Carrie became eligible for the CFO bonus which is directly linked to the \nprofitability of Yoga. \nIn January 20X7 the compliance officer at Yoga received information from a whistleblower regarding \nsuspicions of fraudulent activity perpetrated by Carrie and Jamie, a finance assistant. Jamie’s duties \nusually cover revenue and receivables, but he had been requested to cover some payroll and HR \ntasks during the year due to staff shortages.  \nAfter hearing from the whistleblower, the CEO and the compliance officer engaged Black & Miller to \ninvestigate these suspicions. \nDetails reported by whistleblower  \nThe concerns raised by the whistleblower involved both potential fraudulent financial reporting and \nmisappropriation of assets. \nInformation they provided in support of their claims included: \n• \nCarrie has been overheard boasting about “record revenue” in December 20X6 which \nsurprised the sales team as the increase in members in December was in line with prior \nperiods.  \n• \nOn review of Yoga’s draft financial statements, it seemed unusual that no allowance for \ndoubtful debts had been made. Yoga has several aged receivables, relating to an \narrangement Yoga entered in early 20X6 with a large corporate business which purchased \nannual memberships for a large number of employees. The business was  given 30–days in \nwhich to pay, but no payment has yet been made..  \n• \nApproximately £300k has been capitalised in relation to studio refurbishments. Six studios \nwere refurbished during the year, with a project in place to refurbish ten more in 20X7. The \nproject budget was set at £20,000 per studio. Carrie has had full responsibility for the financial \nmanagement of this project in the CFOs absence.  \n• \nDuring the year three new employees were created in the HR system. However the \nwhistleblower has not seen any evidence of these employees working at Yoga \n• \nCarrie and Jamie were overheard discussing how they will be spending the “additional cash” \nthey’ve made recently, but there have been no staff–wide increases in pay \nAdditional information \nOn review of system access, Carrie has been found to have administrator access to Yoga’s systems, \nallowing her to both post journals and make bank payments without further approval.  \nJamie has been found to have edit access to the HR masterfile spreadsheet, as well as the payroll \nand revenue and receivable systems.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 26 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 75</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-26-case-p075.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-26-case source PDF page 75\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 76</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-26-case-p076.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-26-case source PDF page 76\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [75, 76], "solution": [71, 72, 73]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-12"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-027", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module6", "modules": ["Module6"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 27", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 15, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 77</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-27-case-p077.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-27-case source PDF page 77\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 78</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-27-case-p078.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-27-case source PDF page 78\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 79</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-27-case-p079.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-27-case source PDF page 79\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 74</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-27-a-p074.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-27-a source PDF page 74\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 75</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-27-a-p075.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-27-a source PDF page 75\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 27 (15 marks) \nSinclair’s Limited \nYou are an experienced audit senior working on the audit of Sinclair’s Limited (Sinclair’s), an oat \nbiscuit manufacturer operating in the Scottish Highlands, for the year ending 31 December 20X6. You \nhave been assessing the design and testing the operating effectiveness of internal controls at \nSinclair’s and are now analysing the results of your controls testing.  \nGracie, your audit manager, has asked you to conclude on the level of reliance which can be placed \non internal controls in a few areas:  \n• \nInventory; \n• \nproperty, plant and equipment (PPE); and  \n• \npayroll.  \nThe findings from your controls testing over these areas are presented in Appendix 1. Gracie would \nalso like you to consider the impact which any control deficiencies may have on the substantive \ntesting process. \nQUESTIONS \nQuestion 1 \nUsing Appendix 1, evaluate the results of the controls testing for Sinclair’s and advise on the level of \nreliance that can be placed on each of the controls tested. \n(10 marks) \nQuestion 2 \nExplain the impact of control deficiencies on the substantive testing process. \n(5 marks) \n(15 marks)\nAppendix 1 \n1. Inventory \nPhase: \nReceive goods \nObjective: \nGoods accepted are of appropriate quality and quantity \nBusiness context: \nSinclair’s receives into inventory all raw materials of production \nControl activity(s): \nUpon receipt of all raw materials members of the warehouse team \nperform a quantity and quality check and agree them to the relevant \npurchase orders \nControl testing method: \nThe audit team has conducted site visits throughout the year and \nobserved the receipt of goods into the warehouse. The control testing \nwas designed to confirm that a quantity and quality check is performed \nwith every receipt of goods, and that these details are agreed to the \nrelevant purchase orders \nFindings: \nNo issues were identified on site visits where the warehouse manager \nwas present. \n \nHowever, on two occasions when the warehouse manager was not \npresent the raw materials were accepted and receipted into inventory \nwith no quantity or quality check performed. When you enquired with \nthe warehouse operators regarding this you were told that no training \nhad been provided to them regarding the processes for the receipt of \ngoods. \n2. Property, plant and equipment (PPE) \nPhase: \nReceive asset/transfer ownership \nObjective: \nAll PPE disposals and transfers are identified and the fixed asset \nregister amended accordingly \nBusiness context: \nSinclair’s operate three production line facilities. PPE disposals \ngenerally relate to the disposal of manufacturing equipment if this has \nbecome worn or a more efficient machine has been sourced \nControl activity(s): \nUser departments complete an asset disposal form which is sent to \nthe finance team. The disposal form must be authorised by one of two \nnamed officers, being the operations director or the head of finance. \nControl testing method: \nSelect a sample of disposals made throughout the year and inspect \ncorresponding asset disposal forms to confirm that these have been \nauthorised by the appropriate personnel prior to disposal \nFindings: \nNo issues were identified. \n \nAsset disposal forms existed and were appropriately authorised for all \nsampled disposals.\nAppendix 1 (continued) \n3. Payroll \nPhase: \nWork done and time recorded \nObjective: \nOnly genuine overtime is recorded \nBusiness context: \nFactory employees at Sinclair’s work a standard number of hours per \nweek, as specified in their employment contract. However, ahead of \nperiods of higher sales such as before Burns Night, St Andrew’s Day \nand Christmas staff can be requested to work additional hours as \novertime. \nControl activity(s): \nOvertime hours exceeding 20% of standard hours must be authorised \nby the factory manager before these are processed by payroll, \nevidenced by an electronic signature on timesheets \nControl testing method: \nSelect a sample of overtime payments made throughout the year and \ntrace to supporting timesheets, inspecting to confirm that the factory \nmanagers authorisation is evidenced \nFindings: \nNo issues were identified for samples selected in the months January \n– September. However, in the last quarter of the year the factory \nmanager was off sick and did not authorise overtime hours. \n \nDuring these months, the payroll clerk ran an exception report prior to \nthe processing of payroll showing any timesheets which varied 20% or \nmore from standard working hours. The exception report was then \nreviewed by the Operations Director and signed to evidence her \nauthorisation. \n \nThe audit team inspected these exception reports and confirmed that \nthese were authorised by the Operations Director in the months of \nOctober, November and December, prior to payroll payments being \nmade.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 27 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 77</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-27-case-p077.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-27-case source PDF page 77\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 78</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-27-case-p078.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-27-case source PDF page 78\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 79</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-27-case-p079.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-27-case source PDF page 79\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [77, 78, 79], "solution": [74, 75]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-6"]}, {"id": "aa-aapq-028", "type": "AAPQ", "module": "Module10", "modules": ["Module10"], "source": "ICAS AA | Additional APQ 28", "provider": "ICAS", "marks": 20, "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 80</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-28-q-p080.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-28-q source PDF page 80\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 76</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-28-a-p076.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-28-a source PDF page 76\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 77</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-28-a-p077.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-28-a source PDF page 77\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>", "isHTML": true, "searchText": "Question 28 (20 marks) \nPizza 4 Now Limited  \nIt is April 20X8 and you are an audit manager reviewing audit testing performed by an audit junior in \nrelation to the client Pizza 4 Now Limited (Pizza), a fast-food delivery company based in central \nScotland, for the year ended 31 December 20X7. The company head office and largest operating unit \nare based in Glasgow and a further four units are located in Edinburgh, Falkirk, Cumbernauld and \nLivingston. \nThe audit junior has performed testing over the existence, completeness, accuracy, valuation and \nallocation, classification and rights and obligations assertions. You are performing a review of the \nevidence collected and documented in relation to the following procedures in order to assess whether \nsufficient, appropriate evidence has been gathered. \nProcedures: \n1. Additions: Select a sample of additions from the fixed asset register (FAR) and inspect purchase \ninvoice and bank statements to agree the cost of the asset, date of the addition and nature of the \ntransaction. \n2. Disposals: Select a sample of disposals from the FAR and inspect sales invoice, trade-\nin/scrapping invoice and bank statements to check the asset was disposed of and the amounts \nrecorded are accurate. \n3. Revaluations: Inspect external valuers' reports to agree the valuations and ensure that they \ncomply with the requirements of IAS 16 Property, Plant & Equipment. \nOverall materiality has been set at £100,000, with a trivial threshold of £5,000. \nRequired: \nEvaluate the audit evidence collected by the audit junior and conclude on whether the evidence is \nsufficient and appropriate. \n(20 marks) \nNote – the completeness of the audit work programme does not need to be evaluated at this stage. \nInstead, keep your review to the sufficiency and appropriateness of the evidence gathered.\nAppendix: Documentation of Testing Performed \nAdditions testing: \nAdditions to PPE consist of the purchase of six vehicles and repairs to the Falkirk store. A representative sample of two vehicles has been selected using a \nrandom sampling technique, and the repairs have also been selected due to the nature of this item. \nSample selected for testing \nDocumented evidence \nSample \n# \nSample \ndescription \nAddition \ndate \nSample \nvalue \n(£) \nInvoice # \nInvoice \ndate \nInvoice \naddressed \nto Pizza \n(Y/N) \nInvoice \nValue \n(£) \nVariance \nAgreed to \nbank \nstatement \n(Y/N) \nNotes \n1 \nPurchase of \ndelivery vehicle \n26 invoice \n1258006 \n05/04/X7 \n25,000 \n1258006 \n05/04/X7 \nY \n25,000 \n0 \nY \nNo issues noted \n2 \nPurchase of \ndelivery vehicle \n28 invoice \n1269001 \n07/08/X7 \n13,000 \n1269001 \n07/08/X7 \nY \n26,000 \n13,000 \nY \nPer enquiry with the \nfinance manager, the \nsample item selected \nrelates to a vehicle used by \nboth the Falkirk and \nCumbernauld locations. As \nthese stores have separate \nidentifiers within the \naccounting system, the \naddition has been split to \nrecognise 50% for Falkirk \nand 50% for Cumbernauld. \nThis sample items relates \nto the Cumbernauld \nallocation of the addition. \n3 \nRepairs to \nFalkirk store \nstorm damage \ninvoice 434 \n24/10/X7 \n6,000 \n434 \n24/10/X7 \nY \n6,000 \n0 \nY \nNo issues noted\nDisposals testing: \nPPE disposals consist of four items, being the disposal of two pizza ovens each with a NBV of £40,000, the disposal of refrigeration equipment with a NBV of \n£15,000, and the disposal of an old laptop used for administration with a NBV of £500. A sample of one item has been selected from the population and tested \nbelow: \nSample selected for testing \nDocumented evidence \nSample \nnumber \nSample description \nDisposal \ndate \nSample \nvalue (£) \nInvoice \nnumber \nInvoice \ndate \nInvoice \nValue (£) \nVariance \nAgreed to bank \nstatement (Y/N) \nNotes \n1 \nDisposal of laptop \ninvoice TR0045321 \n03/04/X7 \n500 \nTR0045321 \n03/04/X7 \n500 \n0 \nY \nNo issues noted \nRevaluation testing: \nPer enquiry with the finance manager, revaluations relate to the head office location which was valued by an external property surveyor on 31 November \n20X7. The surveyor has chartered status and has performed the valuation of similar properties in the central belt area. Per an internet search, the property \nsurveyor appears to be married to the finance manager.", "appendix": {"title": "AAPQ 28 | Full case, requirements and exhibits", "html": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 80</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-28-case-p080.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-28-case source PDF page 80\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 81</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-28-case-p081.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-28-case source PDF page 81\" loading=\"eager\"></figure><figure class=\"aa-source\"><figcaption>Source PDF page 82</figcaption><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-aapq-28-case-p082.png\" alt=\"aa-aapq-28-case source PDF page 82\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>"}, "sourcePages": {"question": [80, 81, 82], "solution": [76, 77]}, "sourceFiles": ["sources/S_AA_AAPQs.pdf", "sources/S_AA_AAPQs_Solutions.pdf"], "linkTags": ["aa-module-10"]}];
